ng":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:40:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:19:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Free Fractionally Charged Particles (FCPs) are predicted by some theories beyond or extended to the standard model. FCPs have been widely searched for by underground and space-based experiments based on the assumption of heavy lepton-like particles. However, there is a paucity of research focusing on light-mass FCPs (LFCPs) in the sub-MeV mass range. In this work, we report the LFCPs in primary high energy cosmic rays, based on observational data from the Dark Matter Particle Explorer (DAMPE) satellite. This study utilized ten years on-orbit data of DAMPE to search for LFCPs with a charge of $\\frac{2}{3}~e$. No LFCP candidate was observed. Upper flux limit of LFCPs with a mass of 0.511 MeV$/c^{2}$ and a charge of $\\frac{2}{3}~e$ is determined to be $\\rm 5.0 \\times 10^{-11}\\,cm^{-2}sr^{-1}s^{-1}$ at the $\\rm 90\\%$ confidence level."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:52:03Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:52:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:52:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felipe A.","familyName":"Csaszar","name":"Csaszar, Felipe A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John C.","familyName":"Eklund","name":"Eklund, John C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting the Unitary Actor Assumption: Toward Realistic Aggregation of Individual Preferences in Strategy Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"J.4; G.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"Primary 91B16, Secondary 91B10, 91B14","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:39:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:19:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The long-standing unitary-actor assumption in strategy research -- treating firms as monolithic entities with coherent preferences -- misses that organizations are coalitions of individuals with diverse and often conflicting goals. Although behavioral perspectives have challenged this assumption, the field lacks an operational method for deriving an organizational utility function from the disparate preferences of its members and the specific structures used to aggregate them. We develop a mathematical framework that (i) maps individual utility functions into choice probabilities via a random-utility model, (ii) combines those probabilities using an explicit aggregation structure (e.g., unanimity or polyarchy), and (iii) recovers an organizational utility function that rationalizes the collective behavior. This establishes organizational utility functions as operationally meaningful: they summarize and predict organizational choice, yet are generally not simple averages of members' utilities. Instead, aggregation structures systematically reshape preferences -- unanimity approximates the pointwise minima of underlying utility functions, amplifying risk aversion; polyarchy approximates the pointwise maxima, promoting risk-seeking. We illustrate strategic implications in Cournot competition and principal-agent settings, showing how internal aggregation structures shift competitive and collaborative outcomes. Overall, the framework provides a parsimonious way to retrofit unitary-actor models with behaviorally grounded organizational preferences, reconciling the coalition view of the firm with rigorous and tractable strategic analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"45 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:52:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:52:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:52:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20517","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20517","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20517","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rakshit","familyName":"Trivedi","name":"Trivedi, Rakshit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kartik","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Kartik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David C","familyName":"Parkes","name":"Parkes, David C","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inner Speech as Behavior Guides: Steerable Imitation of Diverse Behaviors for Human-AI coordination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:37:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:19:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective human-AI coordination requires artificial agents capable of exhibiting and responding to human-like behaviors while adapting to changing contexts. Imitation learning has emerged as one of the prominent approaches to build such agents by training them to mimic human-demonstrated behaviors. However, current methods struggle to capture the inherent diversity and non-Markovian nature of human behavior and lack the ability to steer behavior at inference time. Drawing inspiration from the theory of human cognitive processes, where inner speech guides action selection before execution, we propose MIMIC (Modeling Inner Motivations for Imitation and Control), a framework that uses language as an internal representation of behavioral intent. MIMIC employs the novel use of vision-language models as linguistic scaffolding to train a conditional variational autoencoder capable of generating inner speech from observations. A diffusion-based behavior cloning policy then selects actions conditioned on current observations and the generated inner speech. MIMIC enables fine-grained steering of behavior at inference time by conditioning the agent on behavior-specific speech. Experiments across robotic manipulation tasks and human-AI collaboration games demonstrate that MIMIC significantly enhances both behavior diversity and fidelity to human demonstrations while enabling nuanced behavioral steering without training on additional demonstrations. We open source our code and provide pre-trained MIMIC agents and qualitative demos at: https://mimic-research.github.io."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Spotlight paper at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20517","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:52:00Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:52:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:52:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20516","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20516","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20516","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Nagai","name":"Nagai, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akio","familyName":"Tomiya","name":"Tomiya, Akio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroshi","familyName":"Ohno","name":"Ohno, Hiroshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lattice Gauge Theory via LLVM-Level Automatic Differentiation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:36:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We enable the automatic construction of Hybrid Monte Carlo (HMC) forces in lattice gauge theory by performing reverse-mode automatic differentiation at the level of optimized LLVM intermediate representation, making the approach applicable to any language that lowers lattice action code to LLVM. In practice, this means that once the action evaluation routine is implemented, the corresponding HMC force can be generated automatically from the same code path, without deriving or maintaining a separate force routine. The method preserves conventional imperative, in-place implementations and enables a single-source workflow in which forces are generated directly from the action code while inheriting compiler optimizations. We perform end-to-end reverse-mode differentiation of both gauge and Wilson fermion actions. For the Wilson fermion case, we find that the force generated by automatic differentiation achieves performance comparable to a conventional hand-written fermion force implementation. The same differentiation pipeline targets both CPU and GPU backends, providing a practical route to performance-portable force construction for compositional lattice actions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures, 1 table + Supplemental Material"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20516","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20515","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20515","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20515","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rakshith","familyName":"Jayanth","name":"Jayanth, Rakshith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viktor","familyName":"Prasanna","name":"Prasanna, Viktor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FAST-Prefill: FPGA Accelerated Sparse Attention for Long Context LLM Prefill"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:36:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:19:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In long-context large language model (LLM) inference, the prefill stage dominates computation due to self-attention over the complete input context. Sparse attention significantly reduces self-attention computation by limiting each token's interactions to a subset of tokens. The attention sparsity pattern varies across input prompts, and within a prompt, each attention head can follow a distinct pattern. This makes attention sparsity dynamic. The requirement of generating the sparsity pattern, combined with limited data reuse in attention, shifts the prefill compute to being memory-bound. This, in addition to the huge energy requirements for long-context inference on GPU, motivates FPGAs as good candidates for accelerating dynamic long-context inference.\n To tackle these challenges, we propose FAST-Prefill, the first FPGA accelerator for long-context prefill-stage inference with dynamic sparse attention. To efficiently generate sparse indices, we propose a \\textit{fused pipeline unit with a memory-aware execution order} to reduce large tensors and irregular memory accesses. To reduce off-chip memory traffic for accessing the KV cache, we utilize the memory hierarchy to design a \\textit{liveness-driven, dual-tier cache}. For high-throughput matrix multiplication, we design a \\textit{hybrid Matrix Processing Unit (MPU)} with DSPs and bit-plane decomposition using LUTs. We implement FAST-Prefill on Alveo U280 and evaluate it on the Llama and Qwen models (batch size = 1) for context lengths ranging from 4K to 128K tokens. We demonstrate an average speedup of up to 2.5$\\times$ in TTFT and 4.5$\\times$ improvement in energy efficiency over GPU implementation on Nvidia A5000 GPU."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20515","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhuang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiying","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Kaiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baibiao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Baibiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yandong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Design of Skyrmion Bags with Tunable Topology in Symmetry-Broken 2D Lattices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:34:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Magnetic skyrmion bags, as high-order topological swirling spin textures, offer rich fundamental physics and distinct advantages for spintronic applications; however, their realization remains a formidable challenge, especially in two-dimensional (2D) systems. Here, through model analysis, we propose a novel design principle for engineering skyrmion bags with tunable topology in symmetry-broken 2D ferromagnetic lattices. The physics correlates to the delicate interplay of isotropic exchange interaction, Dzyaloshinskii-Moriya interaction and magnetic anisotropy, which can stabilize a rich variety of high-order topological spin states as well as the intriguing skyrmionium with zero topological charge. We further validate this mechanism in monolayer CrInTe2 using first-principles calculations and atomistic spin model simulations, revealing the existence of field-free skyrmion bags. Furthermore, we find that a weak magnetic field triggers a transition to skyrmioniums that exhibit remarkable thermal stability up to 240 K. Our results provide a compelling platform for exploring high-order topological magnetism."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:55Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20513","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20513","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20513","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gavin","familyName":"Levinson","name":"Levinson, Gavin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keith","familyName":"Feldman","name":"Feldman, Keith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Performance to Purpose: A Sociotechnical Taxonomy for Evaluating Large Language Model Utility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:31:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:18:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) continue to improve at completing discrete tasks, they are being integrated into increasingly complex and diverse real-world systems. However, task-level success alone does not establish a model's fit for use in practice. In applied, high-stakes settings, LLM effectiveness is driven by a wider array of sociotechnical determinants that extend beyond conventional performance measures. Although a growing set of metrics capture many of these considerations, they are rarely organized in a way that supports consistent evaluation, leaving no unified taxonomy for assessing and comparing LLM utility across use cases. To address this gap, we introduce the Language Model Utility Taxonomy (LUX), a comprehensive framework that structures utility evaluation across four domains: performance, interaction, operations, and governance. Within each domain, LUX is organized hierarchically into thematically aligned dimensions and components, each grounded in metrics that enable quantitative comparison and alignment of model selection with intended use. In addition, an external dynamic web tool is provided to support exploration of the framework by connecting each component to a repository of relevant metrics (factors) for applied evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20513","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20510","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20510","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20510","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Winstone","name":"Winstone, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maddox","familyName":"Wroblewski","name":"Wroblewski, Maddox","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Forberger","name":"Forberger, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shelby","familyName":"Klomp","name":"Klomp, Shelby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Grudichak","name":"Grudichak, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafaq Gulzar","familyName":"Elahi","name":"Elahi, Shafaq Gulzar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miriam M","familyName":"Florez","name":"Florez, Miriam M","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojie","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter J.","familyName":"Pauzauskie","name":"Pauzauskie, Peter J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew A.","familyName":"Geraci","name":"Geraci, Andrew A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Optomechanical Coin Flip: Wavelength-Modulated, Erbium-Powered Rotations in a Levitated System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:25:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:18:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optical levitation of nano-scale systems offers a pathway to highly sensitive rotation measurements, which are critical for advancing gyroscopic technologies. While prior studies have primarily focused on controlling rotational degrees of freedom of optically levitated particles via modulation of optical power, polarization, and ambient pressure, here we demonstrate wavelength-controlled rotation of the \"coin-flip\" mode in optically levitated NaYF hexagonal prisms doped with erbium by modulating the wavelength of a secondary pump beam. By switching the pump light wavelength, we precisely modulate the particle's rotation rate in a binary fashion, encoding the ASCII message \"hello\" in its rotational frequency. Finally, we observe long-term bimodal periodic dynamics in the rotational motion of a levitated prism that are suggestive of a Dzhanibekov (or tennis-racket)-like effect."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20510","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:48Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Anthony","familyName":"Mason","name":"Mason, William Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Indaleko: The Unified Personal Index"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:17:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:18:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.14288/1.0449905","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personal information retrieval fails when systems ignore how human memory works. While existing platforms force keyword searches across isolated silos, humans naturally recall through episodic cues like when, where, and in what context information was encountered. This dissertation presents the Unified Personal Index (UPI), a memory-aligned architecture that bridges this fundamental gap. The Indaleko prototype demonstrates the UPI's feasibility on a 31-million file dataset spanning 160TB across eight storage platforms. By integrating temporal, spatial, and activity metadata into a unified graph database, Indaleko enables natural language queries like \"photos near the conference venue last spring\" that existing systems cannot process. The implementation achieves sub-second query responses through memory anchor indexing, eliminates cross-platform search fragmentation, and maintains perfect precision for well-specified memory patterns. Evaluation against commercial systems (Google Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, Windows Search) reveals that all fail on memory-based queries, returning overwhelming result sets without contextual filtering. In contrast, Indaleko successfully processes multi-dimensional queries combining time, location, and activity patterns. The extensible architecture supports rapid integration of new data sources (10 minutes to 10 hours per provider) while preserving privacy through UUID-based semantic decoupling. The UPI's architectural synthesis bridges cognitive theory with distributed systems design, as demonstrated through the Indaleko prototype and rigorous evaluation. This work transforms personal information retrieval from keyword matching to memory-aligned finding, providing immediate benefits for existing data while establishing foundations for future context-aware systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"PhD dissertation, University of British Columbia, August 2025. 287 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunlei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The singularity at degenerate points in steady axisymmetric compressible free surface flows with gravity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35R35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:16:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:18:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we analyze the singular shape of the free boundary at degenerate points in a three dimensional axisymmetric compressible gravity flow. For all possible degenerate points on the free surface, we prove that the only nontrivial asymptotic behavior of the free surface at the stagnation points away from the axis of symmetry is the Stokes corner flow. The possible geometries for free boundaries at the non-stagnation axis points are downward pointing or upward pointing cusps. At the origin, there are only two nontrivial asymptotics possible: the Garabedian's pointed bubble or a horizontal flat surface.\n The problem is associated with the analysis of the degenerate points of a quasilinear free boundary problem of the Bernoulli type, and the main obstacles are the absence of a Weiss-type monotonicity formula. To achieve our goal, we establish for the first time monotonicity formulas for quasilinear Bernoulli type free boundary problems. Our formula works both when the equation becomes singular and when the free boundary condition is degenerate.\n Moreover, we establish a new nonlinear frequency formula at the horizontal flat points at the origin and integrate it with the compensated compactness theory for Euler equations, ensuring the strong convergence of variational solutions.\n Our results resolve the Stokes conjecture [Mathematical and Physical Papers, Vol. I., 1880] in a generalized compressible, three dimensional axisymmetric framework. In addition, it can also be realized as a compressible counterpart to Vǎrvǎrucǎ and Weiss [Comm. Pure Appl. Math., (67), 2014]. Our approach is completely new and gives a systematic approach for studying singularities of a singular Bernoulli type quasilinear free boundary problem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"68pages, 11figures, comments are welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20505","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20505","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20505","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiki","familyName":"Kuramoto","name":"Kuramoto, Yoshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Half a century of the theory of synchronization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:12:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:18:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This review offers a retrospective of the development of the theory of coupled oscillators and synchronization over the past half century. Among the various works made by myself during this period, the following three specific works will be focused on, serving as some key points to illustrate the field's evolution. They are the derivation of (1) a simple partial differential equation exhibiting spatio-tempoeral chaos (Kuramoto-Sivashinsky equaiton), (2) a solvable mathematical model describing synchronization phase transition (Kuramoto model), and the discovery of (3) coexistence of coherence and incoherence in nonlocally coupled oscillators (chimera states). It is emphasized that all these works resulted fron the phase reduction of the complex Ginzburg-Landau equation (or its variants), the equation which was derived with a coworker in 1974 from a certain reaction-diffusion model. A quick overview will also be made on how the above three works influenced the subsequent development of the field of coupled oscillators and synchronization. Finally, a few comments will be made on how the methods of dynamical reduction, such as the center-manifold reduction and phase reduction, are crucial for exploring this field in depth. This article is a largely faithful reproduction of the content presented in my award lecture."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, no figure, article submitted to the Statphys 19"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20505","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:41Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20504","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20504","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20504","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Castro","name":"Castro, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Leaños","name":"Leaños, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"O.","familyName":"Rosario","name":"Rosario, O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Corrections to classical results on Independence and Covering numbers of the Splitting graph"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T03:05:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:18:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The splitting graph $S(G)$ of a finite simple graph $G$ was introduced by Sampathkumar and Walikar in 1980~\\cite{SW1980} and has been extensively studied in relation to graph invariants of $G$. In their original work, several formulas relating the independence number and the vertex cover number of $S(G)$ to the corresponding parameters of $G$ were stated and subsequently cited in the literature. In this paper, we show that some of these classical equalities do not hold in general. We present explicit counterexamples disproving the published results concerning independence and vertex cover numbers of splitting graphs. Moreover, we establish the correct formulas and precisely characterize the cases in which the original statements are valid and those in which they fail. These results correct an error that has remained unnoticed for more than four decades and provide a clearer understanding of splitting graphs from the perspective of independence and vertex cover number."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20504","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20500","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20500","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20500","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peisen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Peisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaofeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunlei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Strategy-Supervised Autonomous Laparoscopic Camera Control via Event-Driven Graph Mining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:56:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous laparoscopic camera control must maintain a stable and safe surgical view under rapid tool-tissue interactions while remaining interpretable to surgeons. We present a strategy-grounded framework that couples high-level vision-language inference with low-level closed-loop control. Offline, raw surgical videos are parsed into camera-relevant temporal events (e.g., interaction, working-distance deviation, and view-quality degradation) and structured as attributed event graphs. Mining these graphs yields a compact set of reusable camera-handling strategy primitives, which provide structured supervision for learning. Online, a fine-tuned Vision-Language Model (VLM) processes the live laparoscopic view to predict the dominant strategy and discrete image-based motion commands, executed by an IBVS-RCM controller under strict safety constraints; optional speech input enables intuitive human-in-the-loop conditioning. On a surgeon-annotated dataset, event parsing achieves reliable temporal localization (F1-score 0.86), and the mined strategies show strong semantic alignment with expert interpretation (cluster purity 0.81). Extensive ex vivo experiments on silicone phantoms and porcine tissues demonstrate that the proposed system outperforms junior surgeons in standardized camera-handling evaluations, reducing field-of-view centering error by 35.26% and image shaking by 62.33%, while preserving smooth motion and stable working-distance regulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Robotics (T-RO). 19 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20500","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:33Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lirandë","familyName":"Pira","name":"Pira, Lirandë","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Rebentrost","name":"Rebentrost, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fundamentals of Quantum Machine Learning and Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:56:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum machine learning (QML) sits at the intersection of quantum computing and classical machine learning, offering the prospect of new computational paradigms and advantages for processing complex data. This chapter introduces the fundamentals of QML for readers from both communities, establishing a shared conceptual foundation. We connect the worst-case, adversarial perspective from theoretical computer science with the physical principles of quantum systems, highlighting how superposition, entanglement, and measurement collapse influence learning and robustness. Special attention is given to adversarial robustness, understood as the ability of QML models to resist inputs designed to cause failure. We motivate the study of QML in adversarial settings, outlining distinctions between classical and quantum data and computations when the adversary is a core element. This chapter serves as a starting point to adversarial and robust quantum machine learning in subsequent chapters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This book chapter has been accepted for Springer Nature Quantum Robustness in Artificial Intelligence and will appear in the book: https://link.springer.com/book/9783032111524#accessibility-information"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:32Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20498","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20498","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20498","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fast Algorithms for Exact Confidence Intervals in Randomized Experiments with Binary Outcomes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:53:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We construct exact confidence intervals for the average treatment effect in randomized experiments with binary outcomes using sequences of randomization tests. Our approach does not rely on large-sample approximations and is valid for all sample sizes. Under a balanced Bernoulli design or a matched-pairs design, we show that exact confidence intervals can be computed using only $O(\\log n)$ randomization tests, yielding an exponential reduction in the number of tests compared to brute-force. We further prove an information-theoretic lower bound showing that this rate is optimal. In contrast, under balanced complete randomization, the most efficient known procedures require $O(n\\log n)$ randomization tests (Aronow et al., 2023), establishing a sharp separation between these designs. In addition, we extend our algorithm to general Bernoulli designs using $O(n^2)$ randomization tests."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20498","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20496","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20496","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20496","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jintu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jintu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"HuangXin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, HuangXin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuojie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pip-Stereo: Progressive Iterations Pruner for Iterative Optimization based Stereo Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:51:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While iterative stereo matching achieves high accuracy, its dependence on Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN) hinders edge deployment, a challenge underexplored in existing researches. We analyze iterative refinement and reveal that disparity updates are spatially sparse and temporally redundant. First, we introduce a progressive iteration pruning strategy that suppresses redundant update steps, effectively collapsing the recursive computation into a near-single-pass inference. Second, we propose a collaborative monocular prior transfer framework that implicitly embeds depth priors without requiring a dedicated monocular encoder, thereby eliminating its associated computational burden. Third, we develop FlashGRU, a hardware-aware RNN operator leveraging structured sparsity and I/O-conscious design, achieving a 7.28$\\times$ speedup, 76.6\\% memory peak reduction and 80.9\\% global memory requests reduction over natvie ConvGRUs under 2K resolution. Our PipStereo enables real-time, high-fidelity stereo matching on edge hardware: it processes 320$\\times$640 frames in just 75ms on an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX (FP16) and 19ms on RTX 4090, matching the accuracy of large iterative based models, and our generalization ability and accuracy far exceeds that of existing real-time methods. Our embedded AI projects will be updated at: https://github.com/XPENG-Aridge-AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026 (3D vision track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20496","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:28Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20495","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20495","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20495","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenrui","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Wenrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unveiling Scaling Laws of Parameter Identifiability and Uncertainty Quantification in Data-Driven Biological Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:51:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating high-dimensional biological data into data-driven mechanistic modeling requires rigorous practical identifiability to ensure interpretability and generalizability. However, coordinate identifiability analysis often suffers from numerical instabilities near singular local minimizers. We present a computational framework that uncovers fundamental scaling laws governing practical identifiability through asymptotic analysis. By synthesizing Fisher information with perturbed Hessian matrices, we establish a hierarchical approach to quantify coordinate identifiability and inform uncertainty quantification within non-identifiable subspaces across different orders. Supported by rigorous mathematical analysis and validated on synthetic and real-world data, our framework was applied to HIV-host dynamics and spatiotemporal amyloid-beta propagation. These applications demonstrate the framework's efficiency in elucidating critical mechanisms underlying HIV diagnostics and Alzheimer's disease progression. In the era of large-scale mechanistic digital twins, our framework provides the scaling laws for data-driven modeling in terms of both parameter identifiability and uncertainty, ensuring that data-driven inferences are grounded in verifiable biological reality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"45 pages, 5figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20495","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:27Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20494","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20494","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20494","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhua","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Changhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tieying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tieying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaogang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Gaogang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KairosVL: Orchestrating Time Series and Semantics for Unified Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:50:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Driven by the increasingly complex and decision-oriented demands of time series analysis, we introduce the Semantic-Conditional Time Series Reasoning task, which extends conventional time series analysis beyond purely numerical modeling to incorporate contextual and semantic understanding. To further enhance the mode's reasoning capabilities on complex time series problems, we propose a two-round reinforcement learning framework: the first round strengthens the mode's perception of fundamental temporal primitives, while the second focuses on semantic-conditioned reasoning. The resulting model, KairosVL, achieves competitive performance across both synthetic and real-world tasks. Extensive experiments and ablation studies demonstrate that our framework not only boosts performance but also preserves intrinsic reasoning ability and significantly improves generalization to unseen scenarios. To summarize, our work highlights the potential of combining semantic reasoning with temporal modeling and provides a practical framework for real-world time series intelligence, which is in urgent demand."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20494","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20493","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20493","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20493","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohang","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Xiaohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youliang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Youliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuanjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AWCP: A Workspace Delegation Protocol for Deep-Engagement Collaboration across Remote Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:49:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid evolution of Large Language Model (LLM)-based autonomous agents is reshaping the digital landscape toward an emerging Agentic Web, where increasingly specialized agents must collaborate to accomplish complex tasks. However, existing collaboration paradigms are constrained to message passing, leaving execution environments as isolated silos. This creates a context gap: agents cannot directly manipulate files or invoke tools in a peer's environment, and must instead resort to costly, error-prone environment reconstruction. We introduce the Agent Workspace Collaboration Protocol (AWCP), which bridges this gap through temporary workspace delegation inspired by the Unix philosophy that everything is a file. AWCP decouples a lightweight control plane from pluggable transport mechanisms, allowing a Delegator to project its workspace to a remote Executor, who then operates on the shared files directly with unmodified local toolchains. We provide a fully open-source reference implementation with MCP tool integration and validate the protocol through live demonstrations of asymmetric collaboration, where agents with complementary capabilities cooperate through delegated workspaces. By establishing the missing workspace layer in the agentic protocol stack, AWCP paves the way for a universally interoperable agent ecosystem in which collaboration transcends message boundaries. The protocol and reference implementation are publicly available at https://github.com/SII-Holos/awcp."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 7 figure, tech report of Agent Workspace Collaboration Protocol"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20493","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:24Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuocheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Nuocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ouwen","familyName":"Huan","name":"Huan, Ouwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingzhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mingzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony Q. S.","familyName":"Quek","name":"Quek, Tony Q. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changchuan","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Changchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Wireless Federated Multi-Task LLM Fine-Tuning via Sparse-and-Orthogonal LoRA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:45:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decentralized federated learning (DFL) based on low-rank adaptation (LoRA) enables mobile devices with multi-task datasets to collaboratively fine-tune a large language model (LLM) by exchanging locally updated parameters with a subset of neighboring devices via wireless connections for knowledge integration.However, directly aggregating parameters fine-tuned on heterogeneous datasets induces three primary issues across the DFL life-cycle: (i) \\textit{catastrophic knowledge forgetting during fine-tuning process}, arising from conflicting update directions caused by data heterogeneity; (ii) \\textit{inefficient communication and convergence during model aggregation process}, due to bandwidth-intensive redundant model transmissions; and (iii) \\textit{multi-task knowledge interference during inference process}, resulting from incompatible knowledge representations coexistence during inference. To address these issues in a fully decentralized scenario, we first propose a sparse-and-orthogonal LoRA that ensures orthogonality between model updates to eliminate direction conflicts during fine-tuning.Then, we analyze how device connection topology affects multi-task performance, prompting a cluster-based topology design during aggregation.Finally, we propose an implicit mixture of experts (MoE) mechanism to avoid the coexistence of incompatible knowledge during inference. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed approach effectively reduces communication resource consumption by up to $73\\%$ and enhances average performance by $5\\%$ compared with the traditional LoRA method."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:22Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20491","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20491","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20491","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Katewongveerachart","name":"Katewongveerachart, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Senjaya","name":"Senjaya, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dyonic Kerr-Sen Black Hole's Resonant Scattering: Absorption and Superradiance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:43:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analytically investigate scalar superradiant scattering in the rotating dyonic Kerr-Sen black hole of Einstein-Maxwell-Dilaton-Axion theory. Starting from the separable Klein-Gordon equation for a massive neutral scalar field, we work in the low-frequency and slow-rotation regime and employ the analytical asymptotic matching (AAM) method to compute the reflection coefficient and the associated superradiant amplification factor. Since an exact global scattering solution is not available in this four-charge geometry, the AAM framework enables a controlled analytic treatment of the near-and far-region dynamics. We provide detailed and systematic derivations of the matching procedure leading to the closed-form amplification formula. The superradiant condition is obtained explicitly and we demonstrate that energy extraction occurs exclusively for co-rotating modes satisfying $Ω\u0026lt; m Ω_H$. We show that the presence of electric and magnetic charges suppresses the amplification relative to the Kerr limit, whereas lighter co-rotating scalar fields broaden the superradiant window and enhance the efficiency of rotational energy extraction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20491","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20490","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20490","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20490","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priya","familyName":"Nagpal","name":"Nagpal, Priya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patanjali","familyName":"Kambhampati","name":"Kambhampati, Patanjali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Lattice Disorder and Collective Dipole Coupling Give Rise to Dicke Physics in Perovskite Quantum Dots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Other Condensed Matter (cond-mat.other)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:43:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Halide perovskite quantum dots exhibit cooperative optical phenomena that are absent in conventional semiconductor nanocrystals, including exciton superradiance, superabsorption, and biexciton superradiance within individual dots. Here we develop a microscopic theory that identifies the physical origin of these Dicke effects and establishes how they can be controlled by materials parameters. The central result is that cooperative emission emerges from a competition between collective coupling of optical transition dipoles and lattice-induced disorder, with the balance governed by the Raman-derived phonon spectral density and the excitonic oscillator strength. At elevated temperature, strong Fröhlich coupling and glassy lattice dynamics produce dynamic disorder that suppresses dipole synchronization and yields incoherent emission. Upon cooling, lattice fluctuations freeze and cooperative coherence emerges when the collective coupling exceeds residual static disorder, defining size- and composition-dependent crossover temperatures that we map as phase diagrams. Extending the framework to biexcitons, we show that a confined biexciton constitutes a single correlated charge distribution dressed by a shared lattice configuration, enabling pathway-indistinguishable decay and cooperative radiative enhancement. The theory quantitatively accounts for observed size, composition, and temperature trends in radiative-rate constant ratios and biexciton binding energies, while explaining why full Dicke saturation is not universal. More broadly, the results establish Raman spectral weight and oscillator strength as design parameters for engineering cooperative quantum-optical behavior in quantum materials."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 3 figures. Includes Supplementary Information (separate file)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20490","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:20Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20489","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20489","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20489","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minseop","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minseop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeeun","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Jaeeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanbyeol","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Hanbyeol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kikun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Kikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taekhyun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Taekhyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyerim","familyName":"Bae","name":"Bae, Hyerim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Application of Large Language Models for Container Throughput Forecasting: Incorporating Contextual Information in Port Logistics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:33:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:16:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in generative artificial intelligence (AI) have demonstrated its substantial potential in various fields. However, its application in port logistics remains underexplored. Ports are complex operational environments where diverse types of contextual information coexist, making them a promising domain for the implementation of generative AI and highlighting the urgency of related research. In this study, we applied a large language model (LLM)-a leading generative AI technique-to forecast container throughput, which is a critical challenge in port logistics. To this end, we adopted a state-of-the-art LLM approach and proposed a novel prompt structure designed to incorporate the contextual characteristics of port operations. Extensive experiments confirm the superiority of our method, showing that the proposed approach outperforms competitive benchmark models. Furthermore, additional experiments revealed that LLMs can effectively learn and utilize multiple layers of contextual information for inference in port logistics. Based on these findings, we explore the key constraints affecting LLM adoption in this domain and outline future research directions aimed at addressing them. Accordingly, we offer both technical and practical insights to support the effective deployment of generative AI in port logistics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20489","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20487","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20487","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20487","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sung Joon","familyName":"Maeng","name":"Maeng, Sung Joon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir Hossein Fahim","familyName":"Raouf","name":"Raouf, Amir Hossein Fahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ozgur","familyName":"Ozdemir","name":"Ozdemir, Ozgur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"İsmail","familyName":"Güvenç","name":"Güvenç, İsmail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihail L.","familyName":"Sichitiu","name":"Sichitiu, Mihail L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Height-Dependent Spectrum Activity Measurements and Modeling: A Case Study with FM Radio Bands"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:29:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing demand for wireless connectivity necessitates advanced spectrum modeling to enable efficient spectrum sharing for next-generation aerial communications. While traditional models often overlook vertical variations in signal behavior, this paper proposes a height-dependent propagation model using a helikite-mounted software-defined radio (SDR). We collected extensive measurement data across the 88 MHz to 6 GHz range in both urban and rural environments. As a case study to validate our methodology, we focus on the FM radio band, which allows us to use publicly available transmitter locations and transmit power levels to facilitate comparisons between analytical with measurement results. We identify a clear transition from non-line-of-sight (NLoS) to line-of-sight (LoS) regimes at a specific altitude threshold and propose an altitude-dependent path loss model that incorporates this transition. Our results demonstrate that the proposed model significantly outperforms the standard free space path loss (FSPL) model in complex urban topologies, providing a more accurate framework for altitude-aware spectrum prediction and management across emerging aerial wireless technologies and bands."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20487","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:15Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20486","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20486","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20486","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paras","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Paras","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"YuePing","familyName":"Sha","name":"Sha, YuePing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janet Shufor Bih Epse","familyName":"Fofang","name":"Fofang, Janet Shufor Bih Epse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brayden","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Brayden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jess A.","familyName":"Turner","name":"Turner, Jess A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicole","familyName":"Balay","name":"Balay, Nicole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hubert O.","familyName":"Asare","name":"Asare, Hubert O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela E. B.","familyName":"Stewart","name":"Stewart, Angela E. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erin","familyName":"Walker","name":"Walker, Erin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid LLM-Embedded Dialogue Agents for Learner Reflection: Designing Responsive and Theory-Driven Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:29:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dialogue systems have long supported learner reflections, with theoretically grounded, rule-based designs offering structured scaffolding but often struggling to respond to shifts in engagement. Large Language Models (LLMs), in contrast, can generate context-sensitive responses but are not informed by decades of research on how learning interactions should be structured, raising questions about their alignment with pedagogical theories. This paper presents a hybrid dialogue system that embeds LLM responsiveness within a theory-aligned, rule-based framework to support learner reflections in a culturally responsive robotics summer camp. The rule-based structure grounds dialogue in self-regulated learning theory, while the LLM decides when and how to prompt deeper reflections, responding to evolving conversation context. We analyze themes across dialogues to explore how our hybrid system shaped learner reflections. Our findings indicate that LLM-embedded dialogues supported richer learner reflections on goals and activities, but also introduced challenges due to repetitiveness and misalignment in prompts, reducing engagement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20486","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:14Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20481","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20481","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20481","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Single conjugacy classes of isometries in orthogonal groups over local fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:20:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:16:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"All isometries $σ$ in a quadratic space over a non-archimedean local field of characteristic not 2 satisfying that any isometry $τ$ which is conjugate to $σ$ in the general linear group is conjugate to $σ$ in the orthogonal group are determined. This extends \\cite[Theorem 2.1]{Mil} to arbitary cases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20481","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubhanshu","familyName":"Shekhar","name":"Shekhar, Shubhanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Javad","familyName":"Khojasteh","name":"Khojasteh, Mohammad Javad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ananya","familyName":"Acharya","name":"Acharya, Ananya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Tohme","name":"Tohme, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamal","familyName":"Youcef-Toumi","name":"Youcef-Toumi, Kamal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:16:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The distinctive architectural features of normalizing flows (NFs), notably bijectivity and tractable Jacobians, make them well-suited for generative modeling. Invertible neural networks (INNs) build on these principles to address supervised inverse problems, enabling direct modeling of both forward and inverse mappings. In this paper, we revisit these architectures from both theoretical and practical perspectives and address a key gap in the literature: the lack of theoretical guarantees on approximation quality under realistic assumptions, whether for posterior inference in INNs or for generative modeling with NFs.\n We introduce a unified framework for INNs and NFs based on variational unsupervised loss functions, inspired by analogous formulations in related areas such as generative adversarial networks (GANs) and the Precision-Recall divergence for training normalizing flows. Within this framework, we derive theoretical performance guarantees, quantifying posterior accuracy for INNs and distributional accuracy for NFs, under assumptions that are weaker and more practically realistic than those used in prior work.\n Building on these theoretical results, we conduct extensive case studies to distill general design principles and practical guidelines. We conclude by demonstrating the effectiveness of our approach on a realistic ocean-acoustic inversion problem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"57 pages, 11 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:05Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20479","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20479","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20479","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gefan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Gefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenqi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kwang-Ting","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Kwang-Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Path-Decoupled Hyperbolic Flow Matching for Few-Shot Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:12:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in cross-modal few-shot adaptation treat visual-semantic alignment as a continuous feature transport problem via Flow Matching (FM). However, we argue that Euclidean-based FM overlooks fundamental limitations of flat geometry, where polynomial volume growth fails to accommodate diverse feature distributions, leading to severe path entanglement. To this end, we propose path-decoupled Hyperbolic Flow Matching (HFM), leveraging the Lorentz manifold's exponential expansion for trajectory decoupling. HFM structures the transport via two key designs: 1) Centripetal hyperbolic alignment: It constructs a centripetal hierarchy by anchoring textual roots, which pushes visual leaves to the boundary to initialize orderly flows. 2) Path-decoupled objective: It acts as a ``semantic guardrail'' rigidly confining trajectories within isolated class-specific geodesic corridors via step-wise supervision. Furthermore, we devise an adaptive diameter-based stopping to prevent over-transportation into the crowded origin based on the intrinsic semantic scale. Extensive ablations on 11 benchmarks have shown that HFM establishes a new state-of-the-art, consistently outperforming its Euclidean counterparts. Our codes and models will be released."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20479","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aristidis","familyName":"Vasilopoulos","name":"Vasilopoulos, Aristidis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Codified Context: Infrastructure for AI Agents in a Complex Codebase"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:11:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based agentic coding assistants lack persistent memory: they lose coherence across sessions, forget project conventions, and repeat known mistakes. Recent studies characterize how developers configure agents through manifest files, but an open challenge remains how to scale such configurations for large, multi-agent projects. This paper presents a three-component codified context infrastructure developed during construction of a 108,000-line C# distributed system: (1) a hot-memory constitution encoding conventions, retrieval hooks, and orchestration protocols; (2) 19 specialized domain-expert agents; and (3) a cold-memory knowledge base of 34 on-demand specification documents. Quantitative metrics on infrastructure growth and interaction patterns across 283 development sessions are reported alongside four observational case studies illustrating how codified context propagates across sessions to prevent failures and maintain consistency. The framework is published as an open-source companion repository."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures, companion repository: https://github.com/arisvas4/codified-context-infrastructure, code DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.18746623"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":2,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:02Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anindita","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Anindita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladislav","familyName":"Golyanik","name":"Golyanik, Vladislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taku","familyName":"Komura","name":"Komura, Taku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Slusallek","name":"Slusallek, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Theobalt","name":"Theobalt, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Dabral","name":"Dabral, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SceMoS: Scene-Aware 3D Human Motion Synthesis by Planning with Geometry-Grounded Tokens"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:09:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthesizing text-driven 3D human motion within realistic scenes requires learning both semantic intent (\"walk to the couch\") and physical feasibility (e.g., avoiding collisions). Current methods use generative frameworks that simultaneously learn high-level planning and low-level contact reasoning, and rely on computationally expensive 3D scene data such as point clouds or voxel occupancy grids. We propose SceMoS, a scene-aware motion synthesis framework that shows that structured 2D scene representations can serve as a powerful alternative to full 3D supervision in physically grounded motion synthesis. SceMoS disentangles global planning from local execution using lightweight 2D cues and relying on (1) a text-conditioned autoregressive global motion planner that operates on a bird's-eye-view (BEV) image rendered from an elevated corner of the scene, encoded with DINOv2 features, as the scene representation, and (2) a geometry-grounded motion tokenizer trained via a conditional VQ-VAE, that uses 2D local scene heightmap, thus embedding surface physics directly into a discrete vocabulary. This 2D factorization reaches an efficiency-fidelity trade-off: BEV semantics capture spatial layout and affordance for global reasoning, while local heightmaps enforce fine-grained physical adherence without full 3D volumetric reasoning. SceMoS achieves state-of-the-art motion realism and contact accuracy on the TRUMANS benchmark, reducing the number of trainable parameters for scene encoding by over 50%, showing that 2D scene cues can effectively ground 3D human-scene interaction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 6 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:51:00Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:51:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:51:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Global Existence for Reaction-diffusion Equations with State-Dependent Delay and Fast-growing Nonlinearities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:06:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work aims to study the initial-boundary value problem of the reaction-diffusion equation with state-dependent delay $\\pa_{t}u-Δu=f(u)+g(u,u(t-τ(t,u_t)))+h(t,x)$ in a bounded domain. We establish the global existence of the problem under suitable dissipative-type structural conditions, allowing both nonlinear terms $f$ and $g$ to have arbitrary polynomial growth rates. Another highlight in this work is that, we significantly relax the continuity assumptions imposed on the delay functions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungyeon","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Jungyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Introduction to Strong Alfvénic MHD Turbulence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T02:04:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s41614-026-00214-0","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many astrophysical fluids are magnetized and turbulent. Such fluids can be often described by magnetohydrodynamics (MHD). In this review, we mainly consider MHD turbulence with a strong mean magnetic field whose energy density is greater than or equal to the local kinetic energy density. In these fluids, the MHD waves, especially Alfvén waves, play a dominant dynamical role. Alfvén waves travel along magnetic field lines and collisions of opposite-traveling Alfvén wave packets are essential for turbulence cascade. We focus on strong turbulence regime, where nonlinear interaction during the collision is sufficiently strong and thus one collision is enough to complete turbulence cascade. We will cover the following types of turbulence. First, we review strong Alfvénic MHD turbulence. If the mean magnetic field is very strong, wave packets move very fast and duration of collision is too short to complete turbulence cascade. Even in this case we will show that strong turbulence regime emerges on a small scale. Second, we will consider small-scale MHD turbulence, where interaction of small-scale variants of Alfvén waves (i.e., whistler waves) is important. Third, we review scaling relations in strong relativistic force-free MHD turbulence, where interaction of relativistic Alfvén waves is important. Finally, we briefly discuss scaling relations in compressible MHD turbulence, where interaction of Alfvén waves is still important."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 13 figures; This is the submitted version prior to formal peer review. The accepted version has more contents and is available at Review of Modern Plasma Physics' website; see https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41614-026-00214-0"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongpeng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Zhongpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoxuan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhuoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CGSTA: Cross-Scale Graph Contrast with Stability-Aware Alignment for Multivariate Time-Series Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:58:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multivariate time-series anomaly detection is essential for reliable industrial control, telemetry, and service monitoring. However, the evolving inter-variable dependencies and inevitable noise render it challenging. Existing methods often use single-scale graphs or instance-level contrast. Moreover, learned dynamic graphs can overfit noise without a stable anchor, causing false alarms or misses. To address these challenges, we propose the CGSTA framework with two key innovations. First, Dynamic Layered Graph Construction (DLGC) forms local, regional, and global views of variable relations for each sliding window; rather than contrasting whole windows, Contrastive Discrimination across Scales (CDS) contrasts graph representations within each view and aligns the same window across views to make learning structure-aware. Second, Stability-Aware Alignment (SAA) maintains a per-scale stable reference learned from normal data and guides the current window's fast-changing graphs toward it to suppress noise. We fuse the multi-scale and temporal features and use a conditional density estimator to produce per-time-step anomaly scores. Across four benchmarks, CGSTA delivers optimal performance on PSM and WADI, and is comparable to the baseline methods on SWaT and SMAP."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by DASFAA'26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:49Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Ballini","name":"Ballini, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Muscarnera","name":"Muscarnera, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Fumagalli","name":"Fumagalli, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Scotti","name":"Scotti, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Regazzoni","name":"Regazzoni, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Elimination-compensation pruning for fully-connected neural networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:56:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The unmatched ability of Deep Neural Networks in capturing complex patterns in large and noisy datasets is often associated with their large hypothesis space, and consequently to the vast amount of parameters that characterize model architectures. Pruning techniques affirmed themselves as valid tools to extract sparse representations of neural networks parameters, carefully balancing between compression and preservation of information. However, a fundamental assumption behind pruning is that expendable weights should have small impact on the error of the network, while highly important weights should tend to have a larger influence on the inference. We argue that this idea could be generalized; what if a weight is not simply removed but also compensated with a perturbation of the adjacent bias, which does not contribute to the network sparsity? Our work introduces a novel pruning method in which the importance measure of each weight is computed considering the output behavior after an optimal perturbation of its adjacent bias, efficiently computable by automatic differentiation. These perturbations can be then applied directly after the removal of each weight, independently of each other. After deriving analytical expressions for the aforementioned quantities, numerical experiments are conducted to benchmark this technique against some of the most popular pruning strategies, demonstrating an intrinsic efficiency of the proposed approach in very diverse machine learning scenarios. Finally, our findings are discussed and the theoretical implications of our results are presented."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:47Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramya","familyName":"Ramalingam","name":"Ramalingam, Ramya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Osbert","familyName":"Bastani","name":"Bastani, Osbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Roth","name":"Roth, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prior-Agnostic Incentive-Compatible Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:53:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:15:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In bandit settings, optimizing long-term regret metrics requires exploration, which corresponds to sometimes taking myopically sub-optimal actions. When a long-lived principal merely recommends actions to be executed by a sequence of different agents (as in an online recommendation platform) this provides an incentive misalignment: exploration is \"worth it\" for the principal but not for the agents. Prior work studies regret minimization under the constraint of Bayesian Incentive-Compatibility in a static stochastic setting with a fixed and common prior shared amongst the agents and the algorithm designer.\n We show that (weighted) swap regret bounds on their own suffice to cause agents to faithfully follow forecasts in an approximate Bayes Nash equilibrium, even in dynamic environments in which agents have conflicting prior beliefs and the mechanism designer has no knowledge of any agents beliefs. To obtain these bounds, it is necessary to assume that the agents have some degree of uncertainty not just about the rewards, but about their arrival time -- i.e. their relative position in the sequence of agents served by the algorithm. We instantiate our abstract bounds with concrete algorithms for guaranteeing adaptive and weighted regret in bandit settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:45Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Long-Short Flow-Map Perspective for Drifting Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper provides a reinterpretation of the Drifting Model~\\cite{deng2026generative} through a semigroup-consistent long-short flow-map factorization. We show that a global transport process can be decomposed into a long-horizon flow map followed by a short-time terminal flow map admitting a closed-form optimal velocity representation, and that taking the terminal interval length to zero recovers exactly the drifting field together with a conservative impulse term required for flow-map consistency. Based on this perspective, we propose a new likelihood learning formulation that aligns the long-short flow-map decomposition with density evolution under transport. We validate the framework through both theoretical analysis and empirical evaluations on benchmark tests, and further provide a theoretical interpretation of the feature-space optimization while highlighting several open problems for future study."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20462","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20462","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20462","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Polona","familyName":"Durcik","name":"Durcik, Polona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paata","familyName":"Ivanisvili","name":"Ivanisvili, Paata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joris","familyName":"Roos","name":"Roos, Joris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sharp isoperimetric inequalities on the Hamming cube II: The critical exponent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60E15, 94D10, 05C35, 65G30, 94A17","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:45:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A sharp isoperimetric inequality for the Hamming cube is proved at the critical exponent $β=\\frac12$. This follows up on previous work, where such bounds were established for $β$ near $\\frac12$. As a consequence, this result settles a conjecture of Kahn and Park on cube partitions and yields a sharp $L^1$ Poincaré inequality for Boolean-valued functions. It also confirms a low-noise limit for balanced functions predicted by the Hellinger conjecture on noisy Boolean channels in information theory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages; accompanying code at https://github.com/roos-j/dir24-isoperim"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20462","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:40Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20461","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20461","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20461","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Bingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongtao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhongtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendong","familyName":"XU","name":"XU, Wendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Canini","name":"Canini, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Orabona","name":"Orabona, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yik Chung","familyName":"WU","name":"WU, Yik Chung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ngai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nonparametric Teaching of Attention Learners"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Attention learners, neural networks built on the attention mechanism, e.g., transformers, excel at learning the implicit relationships that relate sequences to their corresponding properties, e.g., mapping a given sequence of tokens to the probability of the next token. However, the learning process tends to be costly. To address this, we present a novel paradigm named Attention Neural Teaching (AtteNT) that reinterprets the learning process through a nonparametric teaching perspective. Specifically, the latter provides a theoretical framework for teaching mappings that are implicitly defined (i.e., nonparametric) via example selection. Such an implicit mapping is embodied through a dense set of sequence-property pairs, with the AtteNT teacher selecting a subset to accelerate convergence in attention learner training. By analytically investigating the role of attention on parameter-based gradient descent during training, and recasting the evolution of attention learners, shaped by parameter updates, through functional gradient descent in nonparametric teaching, we show for the first time that teaching attention learners is consistent with teaching importance-adaptive nonparametric learners. These new findings readily commit AtteNT to enhancing learning efficiency of attention learners. Specifically, we observe training time reductions of 13.01% for LLMs and 20.58% for ViTs, spanning both fine-tuning and training-from-scratch regimes. Crucially, these gains are achieved without compromising accuracy; in fact, performance is consistently preserved and often enhanced across a diverse set of downstream tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 (36 pages, 6 figures)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20461","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:38Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artem V.","familyName":"Bondarenko","name":"Bondarenko, Artem V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Engelhardt","name":"Engelhardt, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marios","familyName":"Kounalakis","name":"Kounalakis, Marios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thierry","familyName":"Valet","name":"Valet, Thierry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerrit E. W.","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, Gerrit E. W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvia Viola","familyName":"Kusminskiy","name":"Kusminskiy, Silvia Viola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaroslav M.","familyName":"Blanter","name":"Blanter, Yaroslav M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Magnetoelastic conversion in integrated YIG nanostructures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Motivated by the recent proposal of two-step transduction from microwave to optical domain using magnetic and elastic intermediate stages arXiv:2205.05088, we consider the coupling between resonant magnetic and elastic modes within a simple axially-symmetric nanodevice designed to host high-quality-factor acoustic modes: A suspended YIG ring structure supported by a central stem, fabricated from a continuous single-crystal film. We study the modes of the system with our custom finite element solvers. We identify the lowest order ``breathing'' mode of a magnetic vortex and the lowest order elastic breathing mode as having the largest mode overlap. For this pair of modes, the external out-of-plane magnetic bias field is critical for bringing them into resonance; however, we show that at the same time it also affects the strength of the coupling. To counteract this, we optimize the radius of the ring at fixed thickness. For the 100 nm-thick film the resonant coupling is maximized at $g/2π= 8\\text{MHz}$ at $R\\approx1.7μ\\text{m}$, indicating that the overlap integral approaches the idealized limit assumed in previous order-of-magnitude estimates. Our results pave the way for the design of tunable frequency-conversion devices based on magnetoelastics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:34Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20457","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20457","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20457","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zimeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mudit","familyName":"Gaur","name":"Gaur, Mudit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaneet","familyName":"Aggarwal","name":"Aggarwal, Vaneet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study online alignment of large language models under misspecified preference feedback, where the observed preference oracle deviates from an ideal but unknown ground-truth oracle. The online LLM alignment problem is a bi-level reinforcement problem due to the coupling between data collection and policy updates. Recently, the problem has been reduced to tractable single-level objective in the SAIL (Self-Improving Efficient Online Alignment) framework. In this paper, we introduce a pointwise oracle uncertainty set in this problem and formulate an oracle-robust online alignment objective as a worst-case optimization problem. For log-linear policies, we show that this robust objective admits an exact closed-form decomposition into the original loss function plus an explicit sensitivity penalty. We develop projected stochastic composite updates for the resulting weakly convex objective and prove $\\widetilde{O}(\\varepsilon^{-2})$ oracle complexity for reaching approximate stationarity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20457","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:33Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20455","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20455","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20455","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monica","familyName":"Lichtenwalner","name":"Lichtenwalner, Monica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiram H.","familyName":"López","name":"López, Hiram H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gretchen L.","familyName":"Matthews","name":"Matthews, Gretchen L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Padmapani","familyName":"Seneviratne","name":"Seneviratne, Padmapani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Permutation decoding of algebraic geometry codes from Hermitian and norm-trace curves"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Commutative Algebra (math.AC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"94B05, 11T71, 14G50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Permutation decoding is a process that utilizes the permutation automorphism group of a linear code to correct errors in received words. Given a received word, a set of automorphisms, called a PD set, moves errors out of the information positions so that the original message can be determined. In this paper, we investigate permutation decoding for certain families of algebraic geometry codes. Automorphisms of the underlying curve are used to specify permutation automorphisms of the code. Specifically, we describe permutation decoding sets that correct specific burst errors for one-point codes on Hermitian and norm-trace curves."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20455","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20453","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20453","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20453","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizum Hanim Ab","familyName":"Halim","name":"Halim, Haizum Hanim Ab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dalila","familyName":"Alias","name":"Alias, Dalila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akmal Zaini","familyName":"Arsad","name":"Arsad, Akmal Zaini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewis Tee Jen","familyName":"Looi","name":"Looi, Lewis Tee Jen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rosdiadee","familyName":"Nordin","name":"Nordin, Rosdiadee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denny Ng Kok","familyName":"Sum","name":"Sum, Denny Ng Kok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IoT-Driven Building Energy Management Systems (BEMS) for Net Zero Energy Buildings: Concept, Integration and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Construction and operating of buildings is one of the major contributors to global greenhouse emissions. With the inefficient usage of energy due to human behavior and manual operation, the energy consumption of buildings is further increased. These challenges highlight the need for improved Building Energy Management Systems (BEMS) integrated with Internet of Things (IoT) and data driven intelligence to enhance energy-efficiency in a building and contribute to Net-Zero Energy Buildings (NZEB) targets. This paper offers four keys contributions: i) a systematic review of IoT enabled BEMS including components, network architecture and functional capabilities, ii) an evaluation of real-world BEMS datasets to support Artificial Intelligence (AI) based predictive control, iii) an analysis of integration challenges related to interoperability, smart grids and net-zero energy strategies, and iv) a case study highlighting global best practices, performances outcomes, and lesson learned for scaling advanced BEMS solutions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20453","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:27Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20451","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20451","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20451","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equivalent genus-2 factorizations of type (4, 3)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Geometric Topology (math.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:19:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The genus-2 fibrations of type (4, 3) found by Baykur-Korkmaz, Hamada, and Xiao are supported on the same total space. In this short note, we show that the Lefschetz fibration structures are the same."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20451","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20450","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20450","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20450","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nihal","familyName":"Balivada","name":"Balivada, Nihal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrey","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Shrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shashank Shreedhar","familyName":"Bhatt","name":"Bhatt, Shashank Shreedhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suyash","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Suyash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heterogeneity-Aware Client Selection Methodology For Efficient Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:19:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated Learning (FL) enables a distributed client-server architecture where multiple clients collaboratively train a global Machine Learning (ML) model without sharing sensitive local data. However, FL often results in lower accuracy than traditional ML algorithms due to statistical heterogeneity across clients. Prior works attempt to address this by using model updates, such as loss and bias, from client models to select participants that can improve the global model's accuracy. However, these updates neither accurately represent a client's heterogeneity nor are their selection methods deterministic. We mitigate these limitations by introducing Terraform, a novel client selection methodology that uses gradient updates and a deterministic selection algorithm to select heterogeneous clients for retraining. This bi-pronged approach allows Terraform to achieve up to 47 percent higher accuracy over prior works. We further demonstrate its efficiency through comprehensive ablation studies and training time analyses, providing strong justification for the robustness of Terraform."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20450","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Hart","name":"Hart, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeonghwan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jeonghwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huimin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Protein Language Models Diverge from Natural Language: Comparative Analysis and Improved Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:18:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern Protein Language Models (PLMs) apply transformer-based model architectures from natural language processing to biological sequences, predicting a variety of protein functions and properties. However, protein language has key differences from natural language, such as a rich functional space despite a vocabulary of only 20 amino acids. These differences motivate research into how transformer-based architectures operate differently in the protein domain and how we can better leverage PLMs to solve protein-related tasks. In this work, we begin by directly comparing how the distribution of information stored across layers of attention heads differs between the protein and natural language domain. Furthermore, we adapt a simple early-exit technique-originally used in the natural language domain to improve efficiency at the cost of performance-to achieve both increased accuracy and substantial efficiency gains in protein non-structural property prediction by allowing the model to automatically select protein representations from the intermediate layers of the PLMs for the specific task and protein at hand. We achieve performance gains ranging from 0.4 to 7.01 percentage points while simultaneously improving efficiency by over 10 percent across models and non-structural prediction tasks. Our work opens up an area of research directly comparing how language models change behavior when moved into the protein domain and advances language modeling in biological domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:22Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20447","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20447","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20447","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Crosby","name":"Crosby, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel S.","familyName":"Pawlowski","name":"Pawlowski, Marcel S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helmut","familyName":"Jerjen","name":"Jerjen, Helmut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detectability of Satellite Planes in Mock Observations of Isolated L* Galaxies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:15:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/stag372","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The existence and prevalence of planar, co-rotating distributions of satellite galaxies around L* host galaxies in the local universe remains a subject of ongoing debate. Despite numerous observational efforts over the past decade, a statistically robust sample of \"satellite planes\" across the diversity of host galaxy environments is lacking. To guide future observing strategies, we construct a controlled suite of mock observations of on-sky positions and line-of-sight (LOS) velocities of isolated L* host galaxies and their satellite systems, based on samples drawn from the Illustris TNG100-1 cosmological simulation to build a statistical sample. In these mock systems, satellite planes are defined by three key parameters: the number of satellites ($N_{\\mathrm{sat}}$), the fraction residing in a thin co-rotating plane ($f_{p}$), and the orientation angle relative to the observer ($θ_{\\mathrm{rot}}$). We evaluate the sensitivity of three observational metrics, $N_{\\mathrm{cor}}$ (number of co-rotating satellites), $b/a$ (projected flattening of the satellite distribution), and $v_\\mathrm{los}$ (mean absolute LOS velocity), to the presence of such planes. Our results show that detection rates are strongly dependent on $θ_{\\mathrm{rot}}$ and $N_{\\mathrm{sat}}$. Satellite planes that are viewed nearly edge-on or face-on, are the most readily detected. In contrast, intermediate orientations and systems with fewer satellites yield low detection success rates. Generally, only satellite planes with $N_{\\mathrm{sat}}\u0026gt;20$ have high chances of being detected. These findings provide a practical framework for prioritising observational targets and designing future surveys aimed at detecting and characterising satellite planes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 12 figures, accepted for publication in the MNRAS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20447","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:19Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingxuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Tingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Janis","name":"Janis, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kalyn Asher","familyName":"Montague","name":"Montague, Kalyn Asher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Eykholt","name":"Eykholt, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhilung","familyName":"Kirat","name":"Kirat, Dhilung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngja","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Youngja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyong","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Jiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adwait","familyName":"Nadkarni","name":"Nadkarni, Adwait","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Human-AI Collaboration in Cybersecurity Competitions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T01:15:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:13:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Capture-the-Flag (CTF) competitions are increasingly becoming a testbed for evaluating AI capabilities at solving security tasks, due to the controlled environments and objective success criteria. Existing evaluations have focused on how successful AI is at solving CTF challenges in isolation from human CTF players. As AI usage increases in both academic and industrial settings, it is equally likely that human players may collaborate with AI agents to solve challenges. This possibility exposes a key knowledge gap: how do humans perceive AI CTF assistance; when assistance is provided, how do they collaborate and is it effective with respect to human performance; how do humans assisted by AI compare to the performance of fully autonomous AI agents on the same challenges. We address this gap with the first empirical study of AI assistance in a live, onsite CTF. In a study with 41 participants, we qualitatively study (i) how participants' perception, trust, and expectations shift before versus after hands-on AI use, and (ii) how participants collaborate with an instrumented AI agent. Moreover, we also (iii) benchmark four autonomous AI agents on the same fresh challenge set to compare outcomes with human teams and analyze agent trajectories. We find that, as the competition progresses, teams increasingly delegate larger subtasks to the AI, giving it more agency. Interestingly, CTF challenges solving rates are often constrained not by model's reasoning capabilities, but rather by the human players: ineffective prompting and poor context specification become the primary bottleneck. Remarkably, autonomous agents that self-direct their prompting and tool use bypass this bottleneck and outperform most human teams, coming in second overall in the competition. We conclude with implications for the future design of CTF challenges and for building effective human-in-the-loop AI systems for security."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:50:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:50:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:50:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Person