Jaime E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thushara G. S.","familyName":"Pillai","name":"Pillai, Thushara G. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc W.","familyName":"Pound","name":"Pound, Marc W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel Requena","familyName":"Torres","name":"Torres, Miguel Requena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denise","familyName":"Riquelme-Vásquez","name":"Riquelme-Vásquez, Denise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Víctor M.","familyName":"Rivilla","name":"Rivilla, Víctor M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Galaxy","familyName":"Salo","name":"Salo, Galaxy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Álvaro","familyName":"Sánchez-Monge","name":"Sánchez-Monge, Álvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miriam G.","familyName":"Santa-Maria","name":"Santa-Maria, Miriam G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rainer","familyName":"Schoedel","name":"Schoedel, Rainer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anika","familyName":"Schmiedeke","name":"Schmiedeke, Anika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Schultheis","name":"Schultheis, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howard A.","familyName":"Smith","name":"Smith, Howard A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiaki","familyName":"Sofue","name":"Sofue, Yoshiaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Testi","name":"Testi, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grant R.","familyName":"Tremblay","name":"Tremblay, Grant R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arianna","familyName":"Vasini","name":"Vasini, Arianna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gijs","familyName":"Vermariën","name":"Vermariën, Gijs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexey","familyName":"Vikhlinin","name":"Vikhlinin, Alexey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena","familyName":"Viti","name":"Viti, Serena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Q. Daniel","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Q. Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Fengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Suinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALMA Central Molecular Zone Exploration Survey (ACES) I: Overview"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:38:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The mass flows and energy cycles within the inner regions of galaxies exert a powerful influence on the evolution of the galaxy population. The centre of the Milky Way is the only galactic nucleus for which it is possible to resolve the physical mechanisms that drive these cycles, namely star formation and feedback, while also tracing global (\u0026gt;100 pc) processes which determine where and when star formation and feedback occur. We present an overview of ACES, the 'Atacama Large Millimeter/submillimeter Array (ALMA) CMZ Exploration Survey', a ~1.5\" angular resolution, 0.2-3 km/s spectral resolution ALMA Band 3 (85-102 GHz), survey of the 'Central Molecular Zone' (CMZ) -- the inner-100 pc of the Galaxy (l = 359.4 deg to 0.8 deg). ACES spectral setup is tuned to observe optimal tracers of the physical, chemical, and kinematic conditions in over 70 spectral features (e.g. HCO+, HNCO, SiO, H40alpha, complex molecules) of the gas in the CMZ, to derive the properties of all potentially star-forming Galactic Centre gas, from global scales (100 pc) to dense ~0.05 pc structures that are expected to host individual star-forming cores, down to sub-sonic (\u0026lt;0.4 km/s) velocity resolution. In this overview paper, we provide the scientific justification for the ACES survey, explain the choice of observational setup, and describe the data legacy products. Finally, we show some of the initial ACES data which highlight the power of ACES' combination of high angular resolution, unprecedented spatial dynamic range, sensitivity, spectral resolution and spectral bandwidth as an illustration of how ACES aims to understand how global processes set the location, intensity, and timescales for star formation and feedback in the CMZ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MNRAS. Website is https://sites.google.com/view/aces-cmz/home and data release is linked from there. Pipeline code is at https://github.com/ACES-CMZ/reduction_ACES/releases/tag/2026-02-18"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:45Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. B. de Melo","familyName":"Junior","name":"Junior, E. B. de Melo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E.","familyName":"Strambini","name":"Strambini, E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Giazotto","name":"Giazotto, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. I. L.","familyName":"de Araujo","name":"de Araujo, C. I. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Impact of magnetic field direction on anti-dot-based superconducting diodes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:37:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The superconducting diode effect (SDE) is a fundamental building block for dissipationless nonreciprocal electronics, yet its microscopic origins in thin films often involve competing mechanisms that remain debated. Here, we demonstrate that the SDE can be engineered in niobium films by patterning macroscopic asymmetric antidots, revealing distinct control mechanisms under in-plane and out-of-plane magnetic fields. We identify two dominant contributions to nonreciprocal transport: edge flux pinning, which governs the low-field and in-plane field regimes via surface-barrier asymmetry, and bulk flux pinning, which drives the high-field response and correlates directly with the geometric asymmetry of the antidots. Supported by time-dependent Ginzburg-Landau simulations and an analytical model, we provide a unified description of these regimes, linking the diode efficiency to the specific pinning landscape. These findings establish a flexible design principle for engineering superconducting diodes with tunable functionality, paving the way for their integration into next-generation quantum and cryogenic circuits."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20337","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20337","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20337","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erdem","familyName":"Sucu","name":"Sucu, Erdem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"İzzet","familyName":"Sakallı","name":"Sakallı, İzzet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aharonov-Bohm Effect for Cooper Pairs in Kerr Spacetime: Gravitomagnetic Phase Shifts from Frame Dragging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:35:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The unification of quantum mechanics and general relativity remains among the most profound challenges in fundamental physics. Here we investigate a novel quantum probe of strong-field gravity: the gravitomagnetic Aharonov-Bohm (AB) effect for Cooper pairs propagating in Kerr spacetime. The frame-dragging induced by a rotating black hole (BH) generates an effective vector potential through the off-diagonal metric component $g_{tϕ}$, which couples to the macroscopic phase of the superconducting condensate. We derive the gauge-invariant AB phase shift $Δθ= (4πm^* Ma/\\hbar)(1/r_2 - 1/r_1)$ for an interferometer with arms at radii $r_1$ and $r_2$, where $m^* = 2m_e$ is the Cooper pair mass and $a$ is the BH spin parameter. Remarkably, the predicted phases reach $|Δθ| \\sim 10^{24}$ radians for Sgr~A* and $\\sim 10^{27}$ radians for M87*, reflecting the enormous gravitomagnetic flux near supermassive BHs. We analyze the dependence on interferometer geometry, demonstrate that tidal disruption of Cooper pairs is negligible at distances $r \\gtrsim 10\\,r_s$, and establish connections to the geometric Berry phase. Although direct experimental realization remains beyond current technology due to the vast distances involved, our framework provides quantitative predictions linking quantum coherence to spacetime curvature, complementing recent observations of gravitational AB phases in atom interferometry."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"40 pages, 12 tables, and 10 figures. Comments are welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20337","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:41Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radoslaw","familyName":"Roszczyk","name":"Roszczyk, Radoslaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pawel","familyName":"Tecza","name":"Tecza, Pawel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Stodolski","name":"Stodolski, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krzysztof","familyName":"Siwek","name":"Siwek, Krzysztof","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Natural Language Processing Models for Robust Document Categorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:33:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This article presents an evaluation of several machine learning methods applied to automated text classification, alongside the design of a demonstrative system for unbalanced document categorization and distribution. The study focuses on balancing classification accuracy with computational efficiency, a key consideration when integrating AI into real world automation pipelines. Three models of varying complexity were examined: a Naive Bayes classifier, a bidirectional LSTM network, and a fine tuned transformer based BERT model.\n The experiments reveal substantial differences in performance. BERT achieved the highest accuracy, consistently exceeding 99\\%, but required significantly longer training times and greater computational resources. The BiLSTM model provided a strong compromise, reaching approximately 98.56\\% accuracy while maintaining moderate training costs and offering robust contextual understanding. Naive Bayes proved to be the fastest to train, on the order of milliseconds, yet delivered the lowest accuracy, averaging around 94.5\\%. Class imbalance influenced all methods, particularly in the recognition of minority categories.\n A fully functional demonstrative system was implemented to validate practical applicability, enabling automated routing of technical requests with throughput unattainable through manual processing. The study concludes that BiLSTM offers the most balanced solution for the examined scenario, while also outlining opportunities for future improvements and further exploration of transformer architectures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 1 fiure, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20335","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20335","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20335","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Dongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nantao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Nantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The $G$-Noncommutative Minimal Model Program"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Category Theory (math.CT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:32:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we study the $G$-equivariant noncommutative minimal model program ($G$-NMMP), as an equivariant generalization of the framework introduced in arXiv:2301.13168. The aim of this program is to construct quasi-convergent paths in the spaces of Bridgeland stability conditions on derived categories of $G$-equivariant coherent sheaves. For finite groups, we employ induction techniques to construct such paths from the non-equivariant setting. In the setting of algebraic group actions, we introduce the notion of $\\mathbb T$-stability conditions to reformulate the proposal, and then we construct quasi-convergent paths for equivariant projective spaces from small quantum cohomology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"46 pages, comments are welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20335","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:38Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengjie","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Chengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaukat","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Shaukat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malaika Din","familyName":"Hashmi","name":"Hashmi, Malaika Din","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian Mathias Thomle","familyName":"Mason","name":"Mason, Sebastian Mathias Thomle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francois","familyName":"Picard","name":"Picard, Francois","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikkel Labori","familyName":"Olsen","name":"Olsen, Mikkel Labori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Peyrucain","name":"Peyrucain, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UAMTERS: Uncertainty-Aware Mutation Analysis for DL-enabled Robotic Software"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:31:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-adaptive robots adjust their behaviors in response to unpredictable environmental changes. These robots often incorporate deep learning (DL) components into their software to support functionality such as perception, decision-making, and control, enhancing autonomy and self-adaptability. However, the inherent uncertainty of DL-enabled software makes it challenging to ensure its dependability in dynamic environments. Consequently, test generation techniques have been developed to test robot software, and classical mutation analysis injects faults into the software to assess the test suite's effectiveness in detecting the resulting failures. However, there is a lack of mutation analysis techniques to assess the effectiveness under the uncertainty inherent to DL-enabled software. To this end, we propose UAMTERS, an uncertainty-aware mutation analysis framework that introduces uncertainty-aware mutation operators to explicitly inject stochastic uncertainty into DL-enabled robotic software, simulating uncertainty in its behavior. We further propose mutation score metrics to quantify a test suite's ability to detect failures under varying levels of uncertainty. We evaluate UAMTERS across three robotic case studies, demonstrating that UAMTERS more effectively distinguishes test suite quality and captures uncertainty-induced failures in DL-enabled software."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 6 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20333","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20333","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20333","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samarth","familyName":"KaPatel","name":"KaPatel, Samarth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofia","familyName":"Nikiforova","name":"Nikiforova, Sofia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giacinto Paolo","familyName":"Saggese","name":"Saggese, Giacinto Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Smith","name":"Smith, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DMCD: Semantic-Statistical Framework for Causal Discovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:29:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present DMCD (DataMap Causal Discovery), a two-phase causal discovery framework that integrates LLM-based semantic drafting from variable metadata with statistical validation on observational data. In Phase I, a large language model proposes a sparse draft DAG, serving as a semantically informed prior over the space of possible causal structures. In Phase II, this draft is audited and refined via conditional independence testing, with detected discrepancies guiding targeted edge revisions.\n We evaluate our approach on three metadata-rich real-world benchmarks spanning industrial engineering, environmental monitoring, and IT systems analysis. Across these datasets, DMCD achieves competitive or leading performance against diverse causal discovery baselines, with particularly large gains in recall and F1 score. Probing and ablation experiments suggest that these improvements arise from semantic reasoning over metadata rather than memorization of benchmark graphs. Overall, our results demonstrate that combining semantic priors with principled statistical verification yields a high-performing and practically effective approach to causal structure learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20333","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:35Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20332","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20332","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20332","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicole","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Nicole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Watson","name":"Watson, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alec","familyName":"Koppel","name":"Koppel, Alec","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumitra","familyName":"Ganesh","name":"Ganesh, Sumitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No One Size Fits All: QueryBandits for Hallucination Mitigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:28:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advanced reasoning capabilities in Large Language Models (LLMs) have led to more frequent hallucinations; yet most mitigation work focuses on open-source models for post-hoc detection and parameter editing. The dearth of studies focusing on hallucinations in closed-source models is especially concerning, as they constitute the vast majority of models in institutional deployments. We introduce QueryBandits, a model-agnostic contextual bandit framework that adaptively learns online to select the optimal query-rewrite strategy by leveraging an empirically validated and calibrated reward function. Across 16 QA scenarios, our top QueryBandit (Thompson Sampling) achieves an 87.5% win rate over a No-Rewrite baseline and outperforms zero-shot static policies (e.g., Paraphrase or Expand) by 42.6% and 60.3%, respectively. Moreover, all contextual bandits outperform vanilla bandits across all datasets, with higher feature variance coinciding with greater variance in arm selection. This substantiates our finding that there is no single rewrite policy optimal for all queries. We also discover that certain static policies incur higher cumulative regret than No-Rewrite, indicating that an inflexible query-rewriting policy can worsen hallucinations. Thus, learning an online policy over semantic features with QueryBandits can shift model behavior purely through forward-pass mechanisms, enabling its use with closed-source models and bypassing the need for retraining or gradient-based adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20332","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:34Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20331","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20331","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20331","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ovidiu Cristinel","familyName":"Stoica","name":"Stoica, Ovidiu Cristinel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No change in Hilbert space fundamentalism"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"History and Philosophy of Physics (physics.hist-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:26:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hilbert space fundamentalism (HSF) states that everything about the physical world is encoded in the Hamiltonian operator and the state vector (as a unit vector, not a wavefunction, which requires additional specification of a configuration space, a position basis, or the position observables). That all structures needed to describe reality, including subsystems, space, fields, emerge from these.\n I show that HSF can't account for our observations that the physical world changes in time."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"3 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20331","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:33Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20330","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20330","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20330","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingcheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhu","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Tianhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengyi","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Shengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klara","familyName":"Nahrstedt","name":"Nahrstedt, Klara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Mingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Circuit Tracing in Vision-Language Models: Understanding the Internal Mechanisms of Multimodal Thinking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:26:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:06:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) are powerful but remain opaque black boxes. We introduce the first framework for transparent circuit tracing in VLMs to systematically analyze multimodal reasoning. By utilizing transcoders, attribution graphs, and attention-based methods, we uncover how VLMs hierarchically integrate visual and semantic concepts. We reveal that distinct visual feature circuits can handle mathematical reasoning and support cross-modal associations. Validated through feature steering and circuit patching, our framework proves these circuits are causal and controllable, laying the groundwork for more explainable and reliable VLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the Findings of CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20330","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:31Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20329","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20329","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20329","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo V. L.","familyName":"Barboza","name":"Barboza, Eduardo V. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean Paul","familyName":"Barddal","name":"Barddal, Jean Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Sabourin","name":"Sabourin, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael M. O.","familyName":"Cruz","name":"Cruz, Rafael M. O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CaDrift: A Time-dependent Causal Generator of Drifting Data Streams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:25:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work presents Causal Drift Generator (CaDrift), a time-dependent synthetic data generator framework based on Structural Causal Models (SCMs). The framework produces a virtually infinite combination of data streams with controlled shift events and time-dependent data, making it a tool to evaluate methods under evolving data. CaDrift synthesizes various distributional and covariate shifts by drifting mapping functions of the SCM, which change underlying cause-and-effect relationships between features and the target. In addition, CaDrift models occasional perturbations by leveraging interventions in causal modeling. Experimental results show that, after distributional shift events, the accuracy of classifiers tends to drop, followed by a gradual retrieval, confirming the generator's effectiveness in simulating shifts. The framework has been made available on GitHub."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper submitted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20329","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20327","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20327","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20327","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yana","familyName":"Rodgers","name":"Rodgers, Yana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisa","familyName":"Schur","name":"Schur, Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Flora","familyName":"Hammond","name":"Hammond, Flora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renee","familyName":"Edwards","name":"Edwards, Renee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Douglas","familyName":"Kruse","name":"Kruse, Douglas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disability, Job Satisfaction, and Workplace Accommodations: Evidence from the Healthcare Industry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:23:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10926-025-10316-0","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Purpose. This paper examines the extent to which job satisfaction, requests for accommodations, and the likelihood of a request being granted vary by disability status. We further analyze whether being granted workplace accommodations moderates the relationship between work satisfaction and disability.\n Methods. We use a novel survey of healthcare workers centered on disability status, perceptions of work experiences, and the provision of accommodations. The data are used in a descriptive analysis and multiple regressions to examine the moderating effect of accommodations on the relationship between disability and indicators related to job satisfaction.\n Results. Results show that people with disabilities have more negative perceptions of their work experiences than people without disabilities. Although people with disabilities are more likely to request accommodations than people without disabilities, they are equally likely to have their requests wholly or partly granted. Regression results indicate that the negative relationships between disability status and most measures of work experience are largely eliminated when accounting for the disposition of accommodation requests. The main exception is turnover intentions, in which the adverse relationship with having a disability does not change even when an accommodation is granted. Partly granting accommodations is helpful only for some metrics of job experience.\n Conclusion. Our paper shows that fully granting accommodations can go a long way to closing the disability gap in job satisfaction between people with and without disabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Journal of Occupational Rehabilitation. arXiv admin note: text overlap with arXiv:2602.17790"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20327","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:27Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20326","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20326","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20326","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ghorbanali","familyName":"Haghighatdoost","name":"Haghighatdoost, Ghorbanali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Poisson Hamiltonian Pontryagin Dynamics and Optimal Control of Mechanical Systems on Lie Groupoids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:21:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a Poisson Hamiltonian formulation of Pontryagin dynamics for optimal control of mechanical systems on Lie groupoids. The reduced dynamics is formulated intrinsically on the dual Lie algebroid endowed with its canonical linear Poisson structure and evolves on its symplectic leaves. The main result of this work shows that symplectic leaves, rather than coadjoint orbits, provide the natural reduced phase spaces for Pontryagin dynamics on Lie groupoids. Under suitable regularity assumptions, we prove the equivalence between the variational formulation of the optimal control problem and the associated Poisson Hamiltonian Pontryagin system, and we show that groupoid invariant Lagrangians lead to reduced optimality conditions of Euler Poincare type. Several mechanical examples, including systems with configuration dependent inertia and local symmetries, are presented to illustrate the theory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20326","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:26Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20324","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20324","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20324","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cathy","familyName":"Shyr","name":"Shyr, Cathy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rory J.","familyName":"Tinker","name":"Tinker, Rory J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas A.","familyName":"Cassini","name":"Cassini, Thomas A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin W.","familyName":"Byram","name":"Byram, Kevin W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rizwan","familyName":"Hamid","name":"Hamid, Rizwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel V.","familyName":"Fabbri","name":"Fabbri, Daniel V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josh F.","familyName":"Peterson","name":"Peterson, Josh F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisa","familyName":"Bastarache","name":"Bastarache, Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An artificial intelligence framework for end-to-end rare disease phenotyping from clinical notes using large language models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Phenotyping is fundamental to rare disease diagnosis, but manual curation of structured phenotypes from clinical notes is labor-intensive and difficult to scale. Existing artificial intelligence approaches typically optimize individual components of phenotyping but do not operationalize the full clinical workflow of extracting features from clinical text, standardizing them to Human Phenotype Ontology (HPO) terms, and prioritizing diagnostically informative HPO terms. We developed RARE-PHENIX, an end-to-end AI framework for rare disease phenotyping that integrates large language model-based phenotype extraction, ontology-grounded standardization to HPO terms, and supervised ranking of diagnostically informative phenotypes. We trained RARE-PHENIX using data from 2,671 patients across 11 Undiagnosed Diseases Network clinical sites, and externally validated it on 16,357 real-world clinical notes from Vanderbilt University Medical Center. Using clinician-curated HPO terms as the gold standard, RARE-PHENIX consistently outperformed a state-of-the-art deep learning baseline (PhenoBERT) across ontology-based similarity and precision-recall-F1 metrics in end-to-end evaluation (i.e., ontology-based similarity of 0.70 vs. 0.58). Ablation analyses demonstrated performance improvements with the addition of each module in RARE-PHENIX (extraction, standardization, and prioritization), supporting the value of modeling the full clinical phenotyping workflow. By modeling phenotyping as a clinically aligned workflow rather than a single extraction task, RARE-PHENIX provides structured, ranked phenotypes that are more concordant with clinician curation and has the potential to support human-in-the-loop rare disease diagnosis in real-world settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20324","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20320","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20320","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20320","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tucker","familyName":"Allen","name":"Allen, Tucker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barry Y.","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Barry Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadine C.","familyName":"Bradbury","name":"Bradbury, Nadine C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Neuhauser","name":"Neuhauser, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Environment-Induced Exciton Renormalization in the Photosystem II Reaction Center"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Protein electrostatics tune excitation energies in the Photosystem II reaction center (PSII-RC), yet a fully quantum-mechanical many-body description of how the surrounding protein environment renormalizes excitons has remained computationally inaccessible. The Bethe-Salpeter equation (BSE) within many-body perturbation theory accurately describes excitonic physics through an explicit electron-hole interaction, but is prohibitively expensive for systems containing thousands of valence electrons. Here, we show that for sufficiently large systems the BSE becomes simpler to solve when treated with modern stochastic sampling techniques, as atomistic interactions self-average. In this regime, the effective electron-hole interaction mediated by the environment is governed by collective $k$-dependent polarization. These insights enable an ab initio study of the PSII-RC in which all six chlorins forming the hexameric dye core are treated explicitly together with a roughly seven Angstrom local protein environment. We directly compare the low-lying optical excitations of the isolated chromophore hexamer (1276 valence electrons) and the protein-dye cluster (3238 valence electrons). For $Q_y$ excitations near 680 nm, inclusion of the protein environment induces polarization-dependent energy shifts, redistributes spectral weight, and alters exciton delocalization and pigment character. Lateral and transverse asymmetries in the low-lying excited states are captured at the BSE level of theory. These results establish that we now have the tools for many-body calculations of biological nanostructures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20320","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20319","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20319","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20319","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent W. S.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Vincent W. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Schober","name":"Schober, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cooperative ISAC for Joint Localization and Velocity Estimation in Cell-Free MIMO Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T20:07:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we explore a cooperative integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) framework that utilizes orthogonal frequency division multiplexing (OFDM) waveforms. Under the control of a central processing unit (CPU), multiple access points (APs) collaboratively perform multistatic sensing while providing communication service in a cell-free multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) system. Achieving high sensing accuracy requires the collection of global sensing information at the CPU, which can lead to significant fronthaul signaling overhead due to the feedback of the sensing signals from each AP. To tackle this issue, we propose a collaborative processing scheme in which the APs locally compress and quantize the received sensing signals before forwarding them to the CPU. The CPU then aggregates the information from all APs to estimate the location and velocity of the targets. We develop a distributed vector-quantized variational autoencoder (D-VQVAE) to enable an end-to-end implementation of this scheme. D-VQVAE consists of distributed encoders at the APs to locally encode the received sensing signals, codebooks for quantizing the encoded results, and a decoder at the CPU for location and velocity estimation. It effectively reduces the amount of data transmitted from each AP to the CPU while maintaining a high sensing accuracy. We employ a collaborative learning-assisted scheme to train D-VQVAE in an end-to-end manner. Simulation results show that the proposed D-VQVAE network outperforms the baseline schemes in sensing accuracy and reduces fronthaul signaling overhead by 99% when compared with the centralized sensing approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20319","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:16Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20314","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20314","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20314","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lauren","familyName":"Vogelstein","name":"Vogelstein, Lauren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vedya","familyName":"Konda","name":"Konda, Vedya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deborah","familyName":"Fields","name":"Fields, Deborah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasmin","familyName":"Kafai","name":"Kafai, Yasmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Morales-Navarro","name":"Morales-Navarro, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danaé","familyName":"Metaxa","name":"Metaxa, Danaé","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rapid Testing, Duck Lips, and Tilted Cameras: Youth Everyday Algorithm Auditing Practices with Generative AI Filters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.5.0; K.3.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:59:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's youth have extensive experience interacting with artificial intelligence and machine learning applications on popular social media platforms, putting youth in a unique position to examine, evaluate, and even challenge these applications. Algorithm auditing is a promising candidate for connecting youth's everyday practices in using AI applications with more formal scientific literacies (syncretic designs). In this paper, we analyze high school youth participants' everyday algorithm auditing practices when interacting with generative AI filters on TikTok, revealing thorough and extensive examinations, with youth rapidly testing filters with sophisticated camera variations and facial manipulations to identify filter limitations. In the discussion, we address how these findings can provide a foundation for developing designs that bring together everyday and more formal algorithm auditing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20314","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:08Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20312","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20312","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20312","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guodong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanshuo","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Huanshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mallesham","familyName":"Dasari","name":"Dasari, Mallesham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"N4MC: Neural 4D Mesh Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:58:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:05:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present N4MC, the first 4D neural compression framework to efficiently compress time-varying mesh sequences by exploiting their temporal redundancy. Unlike prior neural mesh compression methods that treat each mesh frame independently, N4MC takes inspiration from inter-frame compression in 2D video codecs, and learns motion compensation in long mesh sequences. Specifically, N4MC converts consecutive irregular mesh frames into regular 4D tensors to provide a uniform and compact representation. These tensors are then condensed using an auto-decoder, which captures both spatial and temporal correlations for redundancy removal. To enhance temporal coherence, we introduce a transformer-based interpolation model that predicts intermediate mesh frames conditioned on latent embeddings derived from tracked volume centers, eliminating motion ambiguities. Extensive evaluations show that N4MC outperforms state-of-the-art in rate-distortion performance, while enabling real-time decoding of 4D mesh sequences. The implementation of our method is available at: https://github.com/frozzzen3/N4MC."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20312","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:05Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronica","familyName":"Pratt","name":"Pratt, Veronica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason R.","familyName":"Reeves","name":"Reeves, Jason R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David V.","familyName":"Martin","name":"Martin, David V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy B.","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Andy B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Korkus","name":"Korkus, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Edelman","name":"Edelman, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evidence for Sympathetic Flaring in TESS Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:57:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:04:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most flares on the Sun occur at random, but there is a small percentage of \"sympathetic flaring\" -- the triggering of one flare by another. Previously there had been no widespread confirmation of sympathetic flares on other stars. In this work, we developed a new flare detection algorithm that is sensitive to closely-separated and overlapping stellar flares. We applied it to TESS data and discovered ~ 220,000 flares on ~ 16,000 stars, the majority of which are M-dwarfs. The wait time distribution between flares demonstrates an excess of closely-separated flares, relative to expectations from a Poisson process. We attribute this to sympathetic flares, occurring at a rate of between 4% and 9%, which matches the rate seen on the Sun. Our result is the first statistically robust detection of sympathetic flares on other stars, demonstrating a commonality between the Sun and low-mass stars."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 9 figures. Accepted for publication in ApJ"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":2,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John Gunnar","familyName":"Carlsson","name":"Carlsson, John Gunnar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A unified solution framework for truck-and-drone routing problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:56:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:04:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coordinated truck-and-drone routing integrates the high capacity and range of ground vehicles with the flexible routing and speed of drones, enabling simultaneous service. Increasingly applied in last-mile delivery, this synchronization helps reduce completion time and operational costs. To improve its efficiency, various coordination modes between trucks and drones have been proposed. Each mode accommodates diverse operational constraints tailored to particular delivery requirements. In existing work, a slight change in the structural framework or operational characteristics could generate a totally different problem variant, which often requires the design of specialized algorithms. Consequently, under the requirement of maintaining structural validation and adapting to multiple operational features, this paper presents a unified three-phase solution framework based on the Lin-Kernighan-Helsgaun algorithm to solve a wide family of truck-and-drone routing problems. To validate its flexibility and effectiveness, we carry out numerical experiments on three problem variants: the Flying Sidekick Traveling Salesman Problem (FSTSP), the Traveling Salesman Problem with Multiple Drones (TSP-mD), and the Vehicle Routing Problem with Drones (VRP-D), benchmarking each against an effective algorithm. Computational results show that the framework can closely match optimal solutions on small-size instances and even improve the best-known solutions for several medium-size instances. Moreover, additional extensions are discussed to further highlight its versatility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:47:03Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:47:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:47:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangqing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Shangqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshavardhan","familyName":"Kamarthi","name":"Kamarthi, Harshavardhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. Aditya","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, B. Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In-context Pre-trained Time-Series Foundation Models adapt to Unseen Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:48:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:04:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time-series foundation models (TSFMs) have demonstrated strong generalization capabilities across diverse datasets and tasks. However, existing foundation models are typically pre-trained to enhance performance on specific tasks and often struggle to generalize to unseen tasks without fine-tuning. To address this limitation, we propose augmenting TSFMs with In-Context Learning (ICL) capabilities, enabling them to perform test-time inference by dynamically adapting to input-output relationships provided within the context. Our framework, In-Context Time-series Pre-training (ICTP), restructures the original pre-training data to equip the backbone TSFM with ICL capabilities, enabling adaptation to unseen tasks. Experiments demonstrate that ICT improves the performance of state-of-the-art TSFMs by approximately 11.4% on unseen tasks without requiring fine-tuning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20306","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20306","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20306","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Carrara","name":"Carrara, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Hirschvogel","name":"Hirschvogel, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesca","familyName":"Bonizzoni","name":"Bonizzoni, Francesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Pagani","name":"Pagani, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Pezzuto","name":"Pezzuto, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Regazzoni","name":"Regazzoni, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shape-informed cardiac mechanics surrogates in data-scarce regimes via geometric encoding and generative augmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:40:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:04:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-fidelity computational models of cardiac mechanics provide mechanistic insight into the heart function but are computationally prohibitive for routine clinical use. Surrogate models can accelerate simulations, but generalization across diverse anatomies is challenging, particularly in data-scarce settings. We propose a two-step framework that decouples geometric representation from learning the physics response, to enable shape-informed surrogate modeling under data-scarce conditions. First, a shape model learns a compact latent representation of left ventricular geometries. The learned latent space effectively encodes anatomies and enables synthetic geometries generation for data augmentation. Second, a neural field-based surrogate model, conditioned on this geometric encoding, is trained to predict ventricular displacement under external loading. The proposed architecture performs positional encoding by using universal ventricular coordinates, which improves generalization across diverse anatomies. Geometric variability is encoded using two alternative strategies, which are systematically compared: a PCA-based approach suitable for working with point cloud representations of geometries, and a DeepSDF-based implicit neural representation learned directly from point clouds. Overall, our results, obtained on idealized and patient-specific datasets, show that the proposed approaches allow for accurate predictions and generalization to unseen geometries, and robustness to noisy or sparsely sampled inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39 pages, 19 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20306","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onur","familyName":"Beker","name":"Beker, Onur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas René","familyName":"Geist","name":"Geist, Andreas René","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anselm","familyName":"Paulus","name":"Paulus, Anselm","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nico","familyName":"Gürtler","name":"Gürtler, Nico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sylvain","familyName":"Calinon","name":"Calinon, Sylvain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georg","familyName":"Martius","name":"Martius, Georg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Smoothly Differentiable and Efficiently Vectorizable Contact Manifold Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:34:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Simulating rigid-body dynamics with contact in a fast, massively vectorizable, and smoothly differentiable manner is highly desirable in robotics. An important bottleneck faced by existing differentiable simulation frameworks is contact manifold generation: representing the volume of intersection between two colliding geometries via a discrete set of properly distributed contact points. A major factor contributing to this bottleneck is that the related routines of commonly used robotics simulators were not designed with vectorization and differentiability as a primary concern, and thus rely on logic and control flow that hinder these goals. We instead propose a framework designed from the ground up with these goals in mind, by trying to strike a middle ground between: i) convex primitive based approaches used by common robotics simulators (efficient but not differentiable), and ii) mollified vertex-face and edge-edge unsigned distance-based approaches used by barrier methods (differentiable but inefficient). Concretely, we propose: i) a representative set of smooth analytical signed distance primitives to implement vertex-face collisions, and ii) a novel differentiable edge-edge collision routine that can provide signed distances and signed contact normals. The proposed framework is evaluated via a set of didactic experiments and benchmarked against the collision detection routine of the well-established Mujoco XLA framework, where we observe a significant speedup. Supplementary videos can be found at https://github.com/bekeronur/contax, where a reference implementation in JAX will also be made available at the conclusion of the review process."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joyanta Jyoti","familyName":"Mondal","name":"Mondal, Joyanta Jyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multilevel Determinants of Overweight and Obesity Among U.S. Children Aged 10-17: Comparative Evaluation of Statistical and Machine Learning Approaches Using the 2021 National Survey of Children's Health"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:31:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:04:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: Childhood and adolescent overweight and obesity remain major public health concerns in the United States and are shaped by behavioral, household, and community factors. Their joint predictive structure at the population level remains incompletely characterized. Objectives: The study aims to identify multilevel predictors of overweight and obesity among U.S. adolescents and compare the predictive performance, calibration, and subgroup equity of statistical, machine-learning, and deep-learning models. Data and Methods: We analyze 18,792 children aged 10-17 years from the 2021 National Survey of Children's Health. Overweight/obesity is defined using BMI categories. Predictors included diet, physical activity, sleep, parental stress, socioeconomic conditions, adverse experiences, and neighborhood characteristics. Models include logistic regression, random forest, gradient boosting, XGBoost, LightGBM, multilayer perceptron, and TabNet. Performance is evaluated using AUC, accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, and Brier score. Results: Discrimination range from 0.66 to 0.79. Logistic regression, gradient boosting, and MLP showed the most stable balance of discrimination and calibration. Boosting and deep learning modestly improve recall and F1 score. No model was uniformly superior. Performance disparities across race and poverty groups persist across algorithms. Conclusion: Increased model complexity yields limited gains over logistic regression. Predictors consistently span behavioral, household, and neighborhood domains. Persistent subgroup disparities indicate the need for improved data quality and equity-focused surveillance rather than greater algorithmic complexity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:53Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20300","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20300","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20300","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Watson","name":"Watson, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicole","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Nicole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumitra","familyName":"Ganesh","name":"Ganesh, Sumitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Makes a Good Query? Measuring the Impact of Human-Confusing Linguistic Features on LLM Performance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:30:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:04:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) hallucinations are usually treated as defects of the model or its decoding strategy. Drawing on classical linguistics, we argue that a query's form can also shape a listener's (and model's) response. We operationalize this insight by constructing a 22-dimension query feature vector covering clause complexity, lexical rarity, and anaphora, negation, answerability, and intention grounding, all known to affect human comprehension. Using 369,837 real-world queries, we ask: Are there certain types of queries that make hallucination more likely? A large-scale analysis reveals a consistent \"risk landscape\": certain features such as deep clause nesting and underspecification align with higher hallucination propensity. In contrast, clear intention grounding and answerability align with lower hallucination rates. Others, including domain specificity, show mixed, dataset- and model-dependent effects. Thus, these findings establish an empirically observable query-feature representation correlated with hallucination risk, paving the way for guided query rewriting and future intervention studies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20300","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:49Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20298","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20298","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20298","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barry","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Barry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaibhavi","familyName":"Manjarekar","name":"Manjarekar, Vaibhavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathaniel","familyName":"Bleier","name":"Bleier, Nathaniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compute System Organization for High Frequency High Order Wavefront Sensing and Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:26:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Maintaining long-term wavefront stability is critical for the Habitable Worlds Observatory (HWO), which targets contrasts approaching $10^{-10}$ and therefore requires continuous dark-zone maintenance using high-order wavefront sensing and control (HOWFSC). Prior work has advanced HOWFSC algorithms and profiled candidate implementations on radiation-hardened processors, highlighting a substantial gap between the computational demands of LUVOIR-scale HOWFSC and the capabilities of current onboard spacecraft hardware. In this paper, we argue that this gap can be closed by offloading the HOWFSC pipeline to a dedicated co-flying compute satellite at Sun-Earth L2. This approach enables the use of modern, radiation-tolerant high-performance processors without increasing risk to the primary observatory. We show that such an architecture can increase the end-to-end control cadence from the sub-hertz regime typical of radiation-hardened onboard processing or ground-in-the-loop operation to tens and even hundreds of hertz. We evaluate commercial hardware platforms in terms of performance and feasibility, and we propose custom architectures that enable higher control frequencies with significant power consumption reductions. Finally, we outline system-level considerations for co-flying compute, including reliability, satellite integration, and inter-satellite communication constraints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20298","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Zhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhou","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Lingzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gap-Dependent Bounds for Nearly Minimax Optimal Reinforcement Learning with Linear Function Approximation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:25:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study gap-dependent performance guarantees for nearly minimax-optimal algorithms in reinforcement learning with linear function approximation. While prior works have established gap-dependent regret bounds in this setting, existing analyses do not apply to algorithms that achieve the nearly minimax-optimal worst-case regret bound $\\tilde{O}(d\\sqrt{H^3K})$, where $d$ is the feature dimension, $H$ is the horizon length, and $K$ is the number of episodes. We bridge this gap by providing the first gap-dependent regret bound for the nearly minimax-optimal algorithm LSVI-UCB++ (He et al., 2023). Our analysis yields improved dependencies on both $d$ and $H$ compared to previous gap-dependent results. Moreover, leveraging the low policy-switching property of LSVI-UCB++, we introduce a concurrent variant that enables efficient parallel exploration across multiple agents and establish the first gap-dependent sample complexity upper bound for online multi-agent RL with linear function approximation, achieving linear speedup with respect to the number of agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:45Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanru","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wanru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Caccia","name":"Caccia, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhengyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minseon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minseon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Sordoni","name":"Sordoni, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Solve Complex Problems via Dataset Decomposition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:25:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Curriculum learning is a class of training strategies that organizes the data being exposed to a model by difficulty, gradually from simpler to more complex examples. This research explores a reverse curriculum generation approach that recursively decomposes complex datasets into simpler, more learnable components. We propose a teacher-student framework where the teacher is equipped with the ability to reason step-by-step, which is used to recursively generate easier versions of examples, enabling the student model to progressively master difficult tasks. We propose a novel scoring system to measure data difficulty based on its structural complexity and conceptual depth, allowing curriculum construction over decomposed data. Experiments on math datasets (MATH and AIME) and code generation datasets demonstrate that models trained with curricula generated by our approach exhibit superior performance compared to standard training on original datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:43Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamra","familyName":"Nebabu","name":"Nebabu, Tamra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Liang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xiao-Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaijin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huaijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Two-Point Hologram for Everything"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:22:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Known holographic dictionaries, especially AdS/CFT, rely on symmetry matching between the bulk and the boundary. We take a step toward a holographic dictionary with no symmetry requirement and without assuming the geometry being asymptotically AdS. Starting from any interacting Majorana generalized free field on a $(0+1)$d boundary and its two-point function data, we derive a concise analytic formula for the dual $(1+1)$d bulk geometry, borrowing techniques from unitary matrix integral and inverse scattering. Using this formula, we compute the near-horizon curvature, give conditions for positive versus negative curvature, and identify simple boundary models with de Sitter or anti-de Sitter near-horizon duals. We also study the large-$q$ SYK model, finding an unusual temperature dependence of the near-horizon curvature, related to the discrepancy between physical temperature and the ``fake disk'' temperature. We also construct, directly from boundary operators, approximate algebras generated by null translations and boost that become exact at the bifurcate horizon."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages plus appendices"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20291","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20291","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20291","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Bonas","name":"Bonas, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Sinnona","name":"Sinnona, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viviana","familyName":"Siless","name":"Siless, Viviana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel","familyName":"Iarussi","name":"Iarussi, Emmanuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"De-rendering, Reasoning, and Repairing Charts with Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:16:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data visualizations are central to scientific communication, journalism, and everyday decision-making, yet they are frequently prone to errors that can distort interpretation or mislead audiences. Rule-based visualization linters can flag violations, but they miss context and do not suggest meaningful design changes. Directly querying general-purpose LLMs about visualization quality is unreliable: lacking training to follow visualization design principles, they often produce inconsistent or incorrect feedback. In this work, we introduce a framework that combines chart de-rendering, automated analysis, and iterative improvement to deliver actionable, interpretable feedback on visualization design. Our system reconstructs the structure of a chart from an image, identifies design flaws using vision-language reasoning, and proposes concrete modifications supported by established principles in visualization research. Users can selectively apply these improvements and re-render updated figures, creating a feedback loop that promotes both higher-quality visualizations and the development of visualization literacy. In our evaluation on 1,000 charts from the Chart2Code benchmark, the system generated 10,452 design recommendations, which clustered into 10 coherent categories (e.g., axis formatting, color accessibility, legend consistency). These results highlight the promise of LLM-driven recommendation systems for delivering structured, principle-based feedback on visualization design, opening the door to more intelligent and accessible authoring tools."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20291","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:36Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20289","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20289","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20289","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zien","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. M.","familyName":"Shermer","name":"Shermer, S. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oktay","familyName":"Karakuş","name":"Karakuş, Oktay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank C.","familyName":"Langbein","name":"Langbein, Frank C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Sim-to-Real Gap in MRS Quantification: A Systematic Deep Learning Validation for GABA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:16:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Magnetic resonance spectroscopy (MRS) is used to quantify metabolites in vivo and estimate biomarkers for conditions ranging from neurological disorders to cancers. Quantifying low-concentration metabolites such as GABA ($γ$-aminobutyric acid) is challenging due to low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and spectral overlap. We investigate and validate deep learning for quantifying complex, low-SNR, overlapping signals from MEGA-PRESS spectra, devise a convolutional neural network (CNN) and a Y-shaped autoencoder (YAE), and select the best models via Bayesian optimisation on 10,000 simulated spectra from slice-profile-aware MEGA-PRESS simulations. The selected models are trained on 100,000 simulated spectra. We validate their performance on 144 spectra from 112 experimental phantoms containing five metabolites of interest (GABA, Glu, Gln, NAA, Cr) with known ground truth concentrations across solution and gel series acquired at 3 T under varied bandwidths and implementations. These models are further assessed against the widely used LCModel quantification tool. On simulations, both models achieve near-perfect agreement (small MAEs; regression slopes $\\approx 1.00$, $R^2 \\approx 1.00$). On experimental phantom data, errors initially increased substantially. However, modelling variable linewidths in the training data significantly reduced this gap. The best augmented deep learning models achieved a mean MAE for GABA over all phantom spectra of 0.151 (YAE) and 0.160 (FCNN) in max-normalised relative concentrations, outperforming the conventional baseline LCModel (0.220). A sim-to-real gap remains, but physics-informed data augmentation substantially reduced it. Phantom ground truth is needed to judge whether a method will perform reliably on real data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages, 10 figures, 12 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20289","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:33Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20287","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20287","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20287","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfredo Roque","familyName":"Freire","name":"Freire, Alfredo Roque","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel António","familyName":"Martins","name":"Martins, Manuel António","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Regular non-normal modal classicalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:15:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel investigation into the consistency operator ($\\circ$), traditionally associated with paraconsistent logics, as a means of capturing non-normal modal classicalities within the Kripke framework. By semantically reinterpreting $\\circ$ as an operator that distinguishes top and bottom values from other values in the algebra, we extend its applicability beyond paraconsistency into classical and modal logics. We introduce the logic $\\mathcal{B}_4^\\circ$, a four-valued Boolean logic augmented with the consistency operator, and provide a sound and complete axiomatization. Building on this foundation, we extend the semantics to the modal domain using the many-logics modal logic (MLML) framework. Specifically, we construct Kripke frames based on an eight-valued Boolean algebra that contains three distinct four-valued subalgebras, each representing a different world type. Our analysis reveals that the resulting modal logic exhibits a normal local consequence relation alongside a non-normal global consequence relation. Consequently, the characterization of frame properties --such as transitivity, reflexivity, and Euclideanness --deviates from modal logic $K$, requiring novel semantic tools. We further identify new modal formulas in an extended language that capture previously unavailable kinds of accessibility, leading to frame characterizations unattainable in traditional modal frameworks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20287","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristin","familyName":"DeVleming","name":"DeVleming, Kristin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A guide to wall crossing for moduli of varieties"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"14J10, 14J17, 14J29, 14J45","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:15:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There have been major developments in the theory of moduli of varieties in the past decade, essentially settling the construction of moduli spaces of log canonically polarized slc pairs and moduli spaces of K-polystable log Fano pairs. Given the construction of these moduli spaces of pairs $(X, D)$, it is natural to ask how the moduli spaces vary as the coefficients of $D$ are perturbed. This phenomenon is known as wall crossing, the theory of which has been developed in several important cases in the past five years. This semi-expository article is an introduction to moduli of varieties and wall crossing, capturing a portion of the theory developed in the past several years. It also introduces tools and techniques used in explicit computations and examples, applying them in new examples."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, comments welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:29Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20285","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20285","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20285","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amisha","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Amisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muskan","familyName":"Porwal","name":"Porwal, Muskan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kanad","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Kanad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CryptRISC: A Secure RISC-V Processor for High-Performance Cryptography with Power Side-Channel Protection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cryptographic computations are fundamental to modern computing, ensuring data confidentiality and integrity. However, these operations are highly vulnerable to power side-channel attacks that exploit variations in power consumption to leak sensitive information. Masking is a widely used countermeasure, yet software-based techniques often introduce significant performance overhead and implementation complexity, while fixed-function hardware masking lacks flexibility across diverse cryptographic algorithms. In this paper, we present CryptRISC, the first RISC-V-based processor that combines cryptographic acceleration with hardware-level power side-channel resistance through an ISA-driven operand masking framework. Our design extends the CVA6 core with 64-bit RISC-V Scalar Cryptography Extensions and introduces two microarchitectural components: a Field Detection Layer, which identifies the dominant algebraic field of each cryptographic instruction, and a Masking Control Unit, which applies field-aware operand randomization at runtime. This enables dynamic selection of Boolean, affine, or arithmetic masking schemes based on instruction semantics, providing optimized protection across algorithms including AES, SHA-256, SHA-512, SM3, and SM4. Unlike prior approaches relying on static masking logic or software instrumentation, our method performs operand masking transparently within the execution pipeline without modifying instruction encoding. Experimental results show speedups up to 6.80$\\times$ over baseline software implementations, with only a 1.86% hardware overhead relative to the baseline CVA6 core, confirming the efficiency and practicality of CryptRISC."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20285","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:28Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Ermedahl","name":"Ermedahl, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sigrid","familyName":"Eldh","name":"Eldh, Sigrid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristian","familyName":"Wiklund","name":"Wiklund, Kristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Haller","name":"Haller, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cyrille","familyName":"Artho","name":"Artho, Cyrille","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PhantomRun: Auto Repair of Compilation Errors in Embedded Open Source Software"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:13:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continuous Integration (CI) pipelines for embedded software sometimes fail during compilation, consuming significant developer time for debugging. We study four major open-source embedded system projects, spanning over 4000 build failures from the project's CI runs. We find that hardware dependencies account for the majority of compilation failures, followed by syntax errors and build-script issues. Most repairs need relatively small changes, making automated repair potentially suitable as long as the diverse setups and lack of test data can be handled.\n In this paper, we present PhantomRun, an automated framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) to generate and validate fixes for CI compilation failures. The framework addresses the challenge of diverse build infrastructures and tool chains across embedded system projects by providing an adaptation layer for GitHub Actions and GitLab CI and four different build systems. PhantomRun utilizes build logs, source code, historical fixes, and compiler error messages to synthesize fixes using LLMs. Our evaluations show that PhantomRun successfully repairs up to 45% of CI compilation failures across the targeted projects, demonstrating the viability of LLM-based repairs for embedded-system CI pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 5 figures, Mining Software Repositories 2026 (MSR 2026) , Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, 13-14 April 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:27Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Egor P.","familyName":"Berezin","name":"Berezin, Egor P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert T.","familyName":"Zaks","name":"Zaks, Robert T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"German Z.","familyName":"Alekhin","name":"Alekhin, German Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanislav V.","familyName":"Morozov","name":"Morozov, Stanislav V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey A.","familyName":"Matveev","name":"Matveev, Sergey A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two approaches to low-parametric SimRank computation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.1; G.1.3; G.2.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"97N40, 05C50, 05C82, 90B10, 65F99,","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:09:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:03:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we discuss low-parametric approaches for approximating SimRank matrices, which estimate the similarity between pairs of nodes in a graph. Although SimRank matrices and their computation require a significant amount of memory, common approaches mostly address the problem of algorithmic complexity. We propose two major formats for the economical embedding of target data. The first approach adopts a non-symmetric form that can be computed using a specialized alternating optimization algorithm. The second is based on a symmetric representation and Newton-type iterations. We propose numerical implementations for both methodologies that avoid working with dense matrices and maintain low memory consumption. Furthermore, we study both types of embeddings numerically using real data from publicly available datasets. The results show that our algorithms yield a good approximation of the SimRank matrices, both in terms of the error norm (particularly the Chebyshev norm) and in preserving the average number of the most similar elements for each given node."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 3 figures, 1 table, 3 algorithms, 24 references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:24Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20279","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20279","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20279","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elias","familyName":"Furrer","name":"Furrer, Elias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Manschot","name":"Manschot, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Functions for Topological Correlators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:03:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:02:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider correlation functions of topologically twisted, $\\mathcal{N}=2$ supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory with gauge group ${\\rm SU}(2)$ and $N_f\\leq 3$ massive hypermultiplets in the fundamental representation. For a smooth, compact, oriented four-manifold $X$ with $b_2^+\u0026gt;1$, the correlation functions are expressed in terms of a finite set of universal functions. The mass dependence of these functions encodes intersection numbers of the moduli space of instantons. We determine closed expressions for the universal functions by combining techniques of the Seiberg-Witten geometry, $u$-plane integral and the blowup formula. If $X$ is specialised to a complex algebraic surface $S$, the correlation functions can be identified with generating functions of Segre invariants for moduli spaces of sheaves on $S$. We verify that our results agree with the results by Göttsche and Kool for these generating functions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages + appendices"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20279","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:20Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20277","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20277","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20277","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah E.","familyName":"Wolfe","name":"Wolfe, Noah E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Mould","name":"Mould, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Veitch","name":"Veitch, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salvatore","familyName":"Vitale","name":"Vitale, Salvatore","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neural Bayesian updates to populations with growing gravitational-wave catalogs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T19:02:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:02:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As gravitational-wave catalogs grow, they will become increasingly computationally expensive to analyze in their entirety, especially when inferring astrophysical source populations with high-dimensional, flexible models. Bayesian statistics offers a natural remedy, letting us update our knowledge of physical models as new data arrive, without re-analyzing existing data. However, doing so requires the posterior probability density of model parameters for previous observations, which is typically intractable. Here, we use variational neural posterior estimation to rapidly update the inferred population of binary black holes as data are observed in gravitational-wave detectors. We apply this approach to real and simulated catalogs analyzed with both low- and high-dimensional population models, testing the reliability of three update cadences: with new catalogs of sources, month by month during an observing run, and as each new signal arrives. We investigate the success and failure modes of neural sequential updates, finding that the robustness of updating is sensitive to the information contained in each update and that updating is most effective when performed with larger segments of data. We outline one additional scientific application enabled by Bayesian updating: identification of events that are individually informative about the population. Neural Bayesian updates to astrophysical population models also provide efficient likelihood representations for joint analyses with other data, e.g., standard-siren cosmology, and similar methods can be used to perform Bayesian stochastic background searches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14+6 pages, 9+2 figures; comments welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20277","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T02:46:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T02:46:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T02:46:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.20276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.20276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel L.","familyName":"Walker","name":"Walker, Daniel L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Ginsburg","name":"Ginsburg, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashley T.","familyName":"Barnes","name":"Barnes, Ashley T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei-Ying","familyName":"Hsieh","name":"Hsieh, Pei-Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Álvaro","familyName":"Sánchez-Monge","name":"Sánchez-Monge, Álvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Savannah R.","familyName":"Gramze","name":"Gramze, Savannah R.","affiliation":[],"name